\h Exodus
\toc1 Exodus
\toc2 Exodus
\mt1 Exodus
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Israel’s descendants are driven into slavery
\p
\v 1 These are the names of Israel’s descendants who accompanied Jacob when he moved his household to Egypt:
\v 2 Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Judah,
\v 3 Issachar, Zebulun, Benjamin,
\v 4 Dan, Naphtali, Gad, and Asher.
\v 5 \add At that time,\add* Jacob had a total of seventy descendants (including Joseph \add and his sons\add* who were \add already\add* in Egypt.)
\p
\v 6 \add As the years progressed,\add* Joseph and his brothers died—all of that generation,
\v 7 \add but they’d had many children who went on to have their own children and grandchildren,\add* and so they became a large and powerful group \add there in Egypt\add*.
\p
\v 8 \add Eventually\add* a new king came to power in Egypt who had never known Joseph
\v 9 and he told his people, “Listen, those descendants of Israel are becoming more numerous and powerful than us.
\v 10 \add So\add*, let’s deal wisely with them, in case they \add continue to\add* multiply. Then if war was to break out, they could easily take the other side and fight against us, and then leave our country.”
\v 11 Then they appointed overseers to work them in slave gangs, and using them as forced labour, they built the store cities at Piton and Ra’meses for Pharaoh.
\v 12 \add However,\add* the more they oppressed them, the more they multiplied and spread out, and \add the more\add* the Egyptians came to dread the Israelites.
\v 13 So \add they\add* made the Israelites work \add long, hard hours\add*,
\v 14 and they made their lives bitter with the hard slavery. \add They were forced to make\add* mortar and bricks, as well as all kinds of slave labour in the fields—all the work they had to do was \add strenuous and tiring\add*.
\p
\v 15 Then the king of Egypt spoke to Shifrah and Puah, the two Hebrew midwives,
\v 16 saying, “When you assist the Hebrew women as midwives, \add as you sit there on your\add* stool, if you see that it’s a male, do something so he’ll die, but if it’s a female, then let her live.”
\v 17 But the midwives \add were godly women\add* and didn’t do according to what the Egyptian king \add had instructed\add* them, and they let the boys live.
\v 18 So the king called the midwives \add back\add*, and he \add demanded\add*, “Why have you done that and let \add their\add* sons live?”
\p
\v 19 “Because the Hebrew women aren’t like Egyptian women,” the midwives answered. “They’re \add more active\add* and give birth before the midwife \add even\add* gets to them.”
\v 20 So God rewarded the midwives, and \add meanwhile,\add* the people \add continued to increase in number\add* and become more powerful,
\v 21 and because the midwives had \add demonstrated their desire to obey\add* God, he gave them families \add of their own\add*.
\v 22 Then Pharaoh commanded all of his people, “You \add all need to\add* toss every newborn boy into the river, but you can let the girls live.”
\c 2
\s1 The birth of Moses
\p
\v 1 \add During that period,\add* a man from the Levi tribe married a woman who was also a Levite,
\v 2 and she conceived and gave birth to a son. When she saw that he was a \add healthy\add* boy, she hid him away for three months,
\v 3 \add but\add* then she wasn’t able to hide him any longer. \add So\add* she got a basket made from \add woven\add* reeds and plastered it with bitumen and pitch. Then she put the baby in it, and floated \add the basket\add* in the reeds along the riverbank,
\v 4 \add leaving the baby’s\add* sister to watch from a distance to see what would happen to him.
\p
\v 5 \add After a while,\add* Pharaoh’s daughter came down to the river to wash \add herself\add*, and \add she and\add* her young \add attendants\add* were walking along the \add riverbank\add*. She saw the container among the reeds and sent \add one of\add* her slave women to get it.
\v 6 She opened it and saw the baby inside, and wow, he \add started\add* crying. She felt sorry for him and said, “This baby \add must be\add* one of the Hebrew children.”
\p
\v 7 Then his sister \add approached and\add* asked \add the princess\add*, “Would you like me to go and find a Hebrew woman who’ll be able to breastfeed the baby for you?”
\p
\v 8 “\add Yes,\add* go,” answered Pharaoh’s daughter, and the girl went and got the baby’s mother.
\v 9 “Take this baby,” said the \add princess\add*, “and breastfeed him for me, and I’ll pay you \add for doing it\add*.” \add So\add* the woman took the baby and looked after him.
\v 10 \add When\add* the boy had grown \add enough\add*, she brought him \add back\add* to Pharaoh’s daughter and he became her son. She named him ‘Moses’ \add (which means ‘pulled out’)\add* because she said that she’d \add plucked\add* him out of the \add river\add*.
\s1 Moses escapes to Midian
\p
\v 11 \add Later on\add* when Moses was fully grown, he went out to visit \add the Hebrews\add* and saw their \add forced labour\add*, and he saw an Egyptian man beating a Hebrew man—one of his own people.
\v 12 Moses looked around to check that no one was watching, then he hit the Egyptian, \add killing him,\add* then he hid his body in the sand.
\v 13 The next day, he went out \add again\add* and wow, two Hebrew men were fighting each other, and he said to the man in the wrong, “Why are you hitting your \add fellow Hebrew\add*?”
\p
\v 14 “Who made you the ruler and judge over us?” \add the man replied\add*. “Are you \add planning\add* to kill me like you killed \add that\add* Egyptian?” Then Moses was afraid because he realised that what he’d done had \add probably become widely\add* known.
\v 15 \add Indeed,\add* when Pharaoh heard about it, he \add ordered\add* Moses to be killed. \add So\add* Moses had to flee from \add the king\add* and he \add took off east\add* to live in Midian and he stayed near the well.
\p
\v 16 The priest there in Midian had seven daughters, and they would come to the well to draw water out and fill up the troughs there so their father’s \add sheep and goats\add* could drink.
\v 17 Now some male shepherds came along and \add started to\add* shoo their flock away, but Moses got involved and helped them so that their animals could drink.
\v 18 \add When\add* they \add got home\add* to their father Re’uel, he \add asked\add*, “How come you \add got home\add* so early today?”
\p
\v 19 “There was an Egyptian man,” they answered, “who stood up for us against those \add other\add* shepherds. And he even drew \add water\add* for us and gave our flock water to drink.”
\p
\v 20 “Where is he \add now\add*?” he \add asked them\add*. “What’s this—you mean you all just left him there? Go and get him \add so we can give him a meal\add*.”
\p
\v 21 \add Later it turned out that\add* Moses was prepared to live with the man, and \add in due course\add* he gave his daughter Zipporah to Moses \add in marriage\add*.
\v 22 \add When\add* she gave birth to a son, he named him ‘Gereshom’ \add (which means ‘foreigner’)\add* because he said, “I’ve become a foreigner \add living\add* in a foreign land.”
\p
\v 23 \add Eventually\add* Egypt’s king died, \add but\add* the Israelites groaned from the slavery \add they were still under\add* and they cried out, and their cry \add for freedom\add* from slavery went up to God.
\v 24 He heard their groaning and remembered his agreement with Abraham, with Isaac, and with Jacob,
\v 25 and he \add looked down on\add* the Israelites and he \add was concerned about them\add*.
\c 3
\s1 God calls Moses from a burning bush
\p
\v 1 \add One time\add* Moses was shepherding the flock of his father-in-law Jethro or Jethro, the priest at Midian), and he led the flock beyond the wilderness and came to a hill. (\add That was later known as\add* the mountain of God at Horeb).
\v 2 \add While he was there.\add* Yahweh’s messenger appeared to him in a flame coming from the middle of a bush, and as Moses looked, to his surprise \add he saw that\add* the bush was burning in the fire yet not \add actually\add* being burnt up.
\v 3 “I’ve got to go and see this amazing sight,” Moses said \add to himself\add*. “How come that bush isn’t burning up?”
\p
\v 4 When Yahweh saw that he’d left his path in order to look, God called to him from the middle of the bush, “Moses, Moses.”
\p “I’m here,” he replied.
\p
\v 5 “Don’t come any closer,” Yahweh said. “Take off your sandals because the place where you’re standing is HOLY ground.
\v 6 I’m the god of your father and the god of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.” \add So\add* Moses covered hid his face because he was scared to look at God.
\p
\v 7 “I’ve certainly noticed the suffering of my people in Egypt,” Yahweh \add continued\add*, “I’ve heard their cries as the \add slave drivers\add* oppress them—I’m unmistakingly aware of their pain.
\v 8 So I’ve come down to set them free from Egyptian control and to bring them up from \add there\add* to a good and wide land—a land that’s very fertile. \add It’s currently\add* the place of the Canaanites, the Hittites, the Amorites, the Perizim, the Hivim, and the Jebusites.
\v 9 \add Yes,\add* the cry of the Israelites has now \add reached\add* me and what’s more, I’ve seen \add how much\add* the Egyptians are oppressing them.
\v 10 So go now and I’ll send you to Pharaoh and bring my people—Israel’s descendants—out from Egypt.”
\p
\v 11 “Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh,” Moses \add asked\add* God, “and that I should \add be the one to\add* bring out the Israelites out from Egypt?”
\p
\v 12 “I’ll certainly be with you,” God \add replied,\add* “and this will be your sign \add so that you’ll know\add* that I have sent you: when you bring the people out from Egypt, you all will serve God on this \add very\add* hill.”
\p
\v 13 “\add But listen\add*,” Moses told God, “\add If\add* I’m about to go to the Israelites and if I told them, ‘Your ancestors’ god has sent me to you.’ then they’d \add ask\add* me, ‘\add Oh yeah,\add* what’s his name?’ then what would I tell them?”
\p
\v 14 “I AM WHO I AM,” God told Moses. “You’ll \add tell them this\add*: ‘I AM (Yahweh) sent me to you.’ ”
\v 15 Then he \add continued\add*, “This is what you \add need\add* to tell the Israelites: ‘Yahweh, the god of your ancestors, the god of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, sent me to you. This is my name forever, and this is my memorial throughout the generations.’
\v 16 Go and gather the Israelite elders, and tell them, ‘Yahweh, the god of your ancestors, appeared to me, the god of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, to say, “I’ve certainly noticed you all and \add how you’re all being treated\add* in Egypt,
\v 17 and I’ve promised to take you all from your mistreatment in Egypt, away to the land where the Canaanites, Hittites, Amorites, Perizim, Hivim, and the Jebusites \add are currently living\add*—to a land that’s very fertile.” ’
\p
\v 18 Then \add the people\add* will listen to what you tell them, and you and the Israelite elders will go to the Egyptian king and you’ll all tell him, ‘Yahweh, the god of the Hebrews, has met with us. \add So\add* now, please let us go three days’ journey into the wilderness, so we can sacrifice to Yahweh, our god.’
\v 19 \add But\add* I know that the Egyptian king won’t let you go \add until he’s forced to\add*.
\v 20 So \add I will use force\add* and I’ll strike Egypt with \add several supernatural acts\add*, and after those, he’ll let you all go.
\p
\v 21 Then I’ll \add make\add* the Egyptian \add people favour you all in such a way that\add* when you go, you \add won’t leave empty-handed\add*.
\v 22 \add For example,\add* an \add Israelite\add* woman will ask her \add Egyptian\add* neighbour (or from someone staying \add where she lives\add*) for gold and silver items and for clothing. Then you’ll put them on your sons and daughters, and \add in this way\add* you’ll all plunder the Egyptians.”
\c 4
\s1 God gives Moses three miracles
\p
\v 1 “But listen,” Moses responded, “they won’t believe me and won’t listen to \add what I say\add*, because they’ll say, ‘Yahweh never showed himself to you.’ ”
\v 2 “\add Well,\add* what’s that in your hand?” Yahweh \add asked\add*.
\p “My \add shepherd’s\add* staff,” he said.
\v 3 “Throw it on the ground,” Yahweh said. \add So Moses\add* he threw it onto the ground and it became a snake, and \add he quickly moved back away from it\add*.
\v 4 “Pick it up by its tail.” Yahweh told him. \add So\add* Moses picked it up by the tail and it became a staff in his hand \add again\add*.
\v 5 “That’s so that they’ll believe that \add I\add* appeared to you: Yahweh—the god of their ancestors—the god of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.”
\p
\v 6 “\add Now\add* put your hand \add on your chest—\add*inside your robe.” Yahweh added. \add So\add* Moses \add did that\add*, and when he pulled it out again, his hand had gone \add totally\add* white with disease.
\v 7 Then Yahweh said, “\add Now, put\add* your hand \add back in again\add*.” \add So\add* Moses \add did it again,\add* and when he pulled it out, wow, it had \add returned to normal\add*.
\v 8 “So, if they don’t believe you, and \add if the first miracle doesn’t convince them\add*, then they’ll believe \add that second miracle\add*.
\v 9 And then, even if they don’t believe those two miracles and \add won’t believe what you tell them\add*, then you should take some water from the river and pour it onto the dry land. Then the water which came from the river will \add turn into\add* blood \add there\add* on the dry land.”
\p
\v 10 “Oh my master,” Moses responded, “I’m not \add very good at speaking to others—I never have been and still aren’t. I seem to get tongue-tied easily\add*.”
\p
\v 11 “Who \add was it that made people’s mouths\add*?” Yahweh said. “Who makes \add someone\add* mute or deaf, or seeing or blind? Isn’t it me, Yahweh?
\v 12 So get going now, and I’ll look after your mouth and \add show\add* you what you to say.”
\p
\v 13 \add But\add* Moses \add replied\add*, “Oh master, please send \add someone else\add*.”
\p
\v 14 Then Yahweh got angry with Moses and told him, “Isn’t Aaron a fellow Levite and your brother? I know that he can speak well, and \add what’s\add* more, he’ll come \add from Egypt\add* to meet you, and \add when he does, he’ll be very happy\add*.
\v 15 \add He’ll become your speaker\add* and you’ll be able to talk to him and \add tell him what to say\add*. I’ll be \add helping both of you speak\add* and I’ll teach you both what you should do.
\v 16 So \add in that way,\add* he’ll be like a mouth for you and speak to the people for you, and you’ll be like God to him.
\v 17 \add In addition,\add* you’ll have that staff with you in order to do those miracles.”
\s1 Moses returns to Egypt
\p
\v 18 Then Moses went back to his father-in-law Jethro, and told him, “Can I please leave so I can return to my \add relatives\add* in Egypt and see whether they’re still alive.”
\p “Go in peace,” Jethro answered.
\p
\v 19 \add Meanwhile\add* Yahweh had told Moses in Midian, “Leave here and return to Egypt because all those who \add wanted to kill you\add* are \add now\add* dead.”
\v 20 Then Moses took his wife and sons and put them on a donkey and returned towards Egypt \add with them\add*, and Moses took the staff \add that God had turned into a snake\add*.
\p
\v 21 \add On the way,\add* Yahweh said to Moses, “When you return to Egypt, all those miracles that I gave you, you’ll be able to \add demonstrate\add* them in front of Pharaoh, but as for me, I’m going to make him stubborn \add so that\add* he won’t let your people leave.
\v 22 \add But\add* tell Pharaoh, ‘Yahweh has stated that Israel is \add like\add* his oldest son
\v 23 and he asked you to let that son go so that he can serve him. But you refused to let Israel go, so Yahweh will kill \em your\em* oldest son.’ ”
\p
\v 24 \add One night\add* as they were \add overnighting at\add* a lodging place near the road, Yahweh \add confronted Moses\add* and would have killed him,
\v 25 \add but his wife\add* Zipporah took a flint \add knife\add* and cut off her son’s foreskin and \add threw\add* it at Moses’s feet saying, “You’re definitely a bloody \add husband\add* to me.”
\v 26 (That was referring to circumcision.) \add So\add* then Yahweh \add left them alone\add*.
\p
\v 27 \add Meanwhile\add* Yahweh had told Aaron, “Go \add out\add* into the wilderness to meet Moses.” \add So\add* he went and met him at the hill of God, and he kissed him.
\v 28 Then Moses told Aaron \add everything\add* that Yahweh had told him and how he had sent him, and all the miracles that he commanded him \add to demonstrate\add*.
\p
\v 29 Then Moses and Aaron went \add to Egypt\add* and they assembled all the \add Israelite\add* elders
\v 30 and Aaron told them \add everything\add* that Yahweh had spoken to Moses. Then Moses did the miracles in front of \add them\add*,
\v 31 and the people believed. Then they heard how Yahweh had paid attention to the Israelites and that he had seen their suffering, and they bowed and prostrated themselves \add in thankfulness\add*.
\c 5
\s1 Moses challenges the Egyptian king
\p
\v 1 After that, Moses and Aaron went in and said to Pharaoh, “This is what Israel’s god Yahweh says: ‘Let my people go, \add so\add* they can celebrate me \add out\add* in the wilderness.’ ”
\p
\v 2 \add But the king\add* said, “Who’s \add this\add* ‘Yahweh’ that I should listen to \add him telling me\add* to let Israel go? I don’t know Yahweh, and what’s more, I won’t let \add the Israelites\add* go \add anywhere\add*.”
\p
\v 3 “The god of \add us\add* Hebrews has met with us,” they replied, “Please, let us go three days into the wilderness so we can sacrifice to our god Yahweh, \add because we don’t want him to strike\add* us with deadly diseases or with \add war\add*.”
\p
\v 4 “Moses and Aaron,” the king of Egypt answered, “why are you causing the people to \add slacken\add* off from their tasks? \add Get back\add* to your \add work\add*.”
\v 5 And Pharaoh \add continued\add*, “Listen, there’s many \add of you\add* and you \add two\add* have caused them to rest from their \add duties\add*.”
\p
\v 6 Then on that \add very same\add* day, Pharaoh ordered the people’s slave masters and supervisors,
\v 7 “Don’t give straw to the people to make bricks with as \add you’ve done up till now\add*. They can go and gather straw for themselves.
\v 8 But \add keep\add* the \add same\add* quota of bricks \add as before\add*—don’t decrease it because they \add must be\add* idle \add if they have time to say\add*, ‘Let us go—let us sacrifice to our god.’
\v 9 \add Give the men more work so they’ll be too busy to worry about deceptive words\add*.”
\p
\v 10 So the slave masters and supervisors of the people went out and told \add them all\add*, “\add The king\add* has said that he won’t give you any more straw,
\v 11 \add so\add* you’ll \add all have to\add* go and get your own straw from wherever you can find it, because there’ll be no reduction to your \add quota\add*.”
\v 12 So the people dispersed throughout all of Egypt to gather stubble for straw,
\v 13 and the taskmasters were \add hassling\add* them, saying, “\add Hurry up and\add* finish your day’s work, the same as when \add you were given\add* straw.”
\v 14 Then the Israelite foremen that Pharaoh’s slave masters had appointed were beaten and told, “Why haven’t you met your required brick making \add quota\add* yesterday or today? \add It’s just the same as in the past\add*.”
\p
\v 15 So the Israelite foremen went and complained to Pharaoh, “Why are you treating your servants like this?
\v 16 We haven’t been given any straw and yet they’re telling us to keep making bricks. And see, we your servants get beaten, but it’s your people’s mistake.”
\p
\v 17 “You’re all lazy—plain lazy!” \add the king responded\add*. “That’s why you’re saying, ‘Let us go sacrifice to Yahweh.’
\v 18 So go now! \add Back to\add* work! And you won’t be given straw, but you \add still have to meet the quota\add* of bricks.”
\p
\v 19 \add When\add* the Israelite foremen heard that the quota wasn’t going to be reduced, they \add realised how serious\add* their \add problem was\add*,
\v 20 so when they left Pharaoh they \add confronted\add* Moses and Aaron who were waiting to meet them,
\v 21 “May Yahweh \add see what you’ve done and judge you for it\add* because you’ve \add raised a big stink in Pharaoh’s mind\add* and with his servants. \add Now they have their excuse\add* to kill us!”
\s1 Moses complains to God
\p
\v 22 Then Moses went back to Yahweh and \add asked\add*, “My master, why have you \add taken it out on\add* our people? Was that why you sent me?
\v 23 \add Eber\add* since I went to Pharaoh to \add deliver your message\add*, he’s \add made\add* these people \add suffer\add* and you \add clearly\add* haven’t rescued \add them\add*.”
\c 6
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh said to Moses, “Now you’ll see what I’ll do to Pharaoh. I’ll use my power to force him to let them go, and then using my power again he’ll \add actually\add* drive them out of his \add country\add*.”
\s1 God promises to rescue the people
\p
\v 2 Then God spoke to Moses \add again\add*, “I am Yahweh
\v 3 and I \add revealed myself\add* to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob as El Shaddai (God the provider) but they didn’t know me by my name Yahweh.
\v 4 \add What’s more,\add* I made an agreement with them: to give them the Canaan region—the area which they had been staying in.
\v 5 \add Furthermore,\add* I’ve heard the complaints from the Israelites who have been enslaved by the Egyptians, and I have remembered my agreement.
\v 6 Therefore, tell the Israelites, ‘I am Yahweh and I will \add help you escape from\add* the \add control\add* of the Egyptians and rescue you from their slavery. I’ll buy you out of slavery using my power and by \add punishing the Egyptians for this injustice\add*.
\v 7 I’ll take you all to be my own people, and I’ll be your god. Then you’ll know that I am Yahweh your god—the one rescuing you all from the Egyptian oppression.
\v 8 Then I’ll \add take\add* you all to the land that I \add promised\add* to give to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob, and I’ll give it to you as \add your own\add*. I am Yahweh.’ ”
\v 9 So Moses \add passed all that on\add* to the Israelites, but they wouldn’t listen to Moses, because they were \add both discouraged and worn out from the slavery\add*.
\p
\v 10 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 11 “Go and speak to the Egyptian king Pharaoh and \add tell him\add* that he needs to let the Israelites leave his country.”
\v 12 And Moses said in front of Yahweh, “Look, the Israelites haven’t listened to me, so why would Pharaoh listen? And I have \add difficulty speaking\add*.”
\v 13 Then Yahweh spoke to Moses and Aaron, and he gave them a command to \add pass on to\add* the Israelites and to the Egyptian king Pharaoh: to \add take\add* the Israelites out of Egypt.
\s1 The ancestors of Moses and Aaron
\p
\v 14 The following were the leaders of the clans:
\p The sons of \bd Reuben\bd* (Israel’s eldest son) were Hanok, Palu, Hezron, and Karmi, and \add they headed\add* the clans of Reuben.
\p
\v 15 \bd Simeon’s\bd* sons were Yemuel, Yamin, Ohad, Yakin, Tsohar, and Shaul—the son of a Canaanite woman, and \add they headed\add* the clans of Simeon.
\p
\v 16 \bd Levi’s\bd* sons were Gereshon, Kohat, and Merari. Levi was 137 years old when he died.
\v 17 Gereshon’s sons were Livni and Shimei, and \add they headed\add* their clans.
\v 18 Kohat’s sons were Amram, Yitshar, Hebron, and Uziel. Kohat was 133 years old when he died.
\v 19 Merari’s sons were Mahli and Mushi, and \add all of those headed\add* the clans of Levi.
\v 20 Amram married his aunt Yokeved, and she gave birth to Aaron and Moses. Amram lived to be 137 years old.
\v 21 Yitshar’s sons were Korah, Nefeg, and Zikri.
\v 22 Uziel’s sons were Mishael, Elitsafan, and Sitri.
\p
\v 23 Aaron \add married\add* Elisheva, Amminadab’s daughter and the sister of Nahshon, and she gave birth to Nadav, Avihu, Eleazar, and Itamar.
\v 24 Korah’s sons were Asir, Elkanah, and Aviasaf, and \add they headed\add* the clans of Korah.
\v 25 Aaron’s son Eleazar took a wife for himself from the daughters of Putiel and she gave birth to Pinhas.
\p \add All of\add* those \add headed\add* the clans of Levi.
\p
\v 26 It was to Aaron and Moses that Yahweh had said, “Bring the Israelites out of Egypt by their \add tribes\add*.”
\v 27 They were the ones who spoke to the Egyptian king Pharaoh, to bring the Israelites out of Egypt.
\s1 God’s reassurances to Moses and Aaron
\p
\v 28 On the day that Yahweh spoke to Moses in Egypt,
\v 29 he said, “I am Yahweh. Tell the Egyptian king Pharaoh everything that I tell you.”
\p
\v 30 “Please listen to me,” Moses responded. “I’m \add not a good speaker\add*, so why should Pharaoh listen to me?”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 Yahweh answered, “Listen, I’ve made you \add like\add* God to Pharaoh, and your brother Aaron will be your prophet.
\v 2 You’ll \add quietly\add* say everything that I tell you to and then your brother Aaron will \add relay it\add* to Pharaoh \add and tell him\add* that he must let the Israelites leave his country.
\v 3 \add But\add* I’ll \add make\add* Pharaoh \add stubborn\add* and \add so\add* I’ll increase the signs and miracles \add that I’ll do\add* in Egypt.
\v 4 Pharaoh won’t listen to you so I’ll \add pressure\add* Egypt, and I’ll use severe judgements \add to ensure that I\add* bring my armies—my people the Israelites—out Egypt.
\v 5 The Egyptians will know that I am Yahweh when I demonstrate my power to Egypt \add by taking\add* the Israelites out from the middle of them.”
\v 6 So Moses and Aaron did everything that Yahweh told them to do.
\v 7 (Moses was eighty and Aaron was eighty-three when they were \add interacting with\add* Pharaoh.)
\s1 Staffs and snakes
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron,
\v 9 “\add When\add* Pharaoh tells you to perform a miracle \add as a sign\add*, then \add just\add* tell Aaron to take his\f + \fr 7:9 \ft It’s not clear here in this account, or even in the following events, if Moses and Aaron had separate miraculous sticks, or if Moses had (temporarily?) entrusted his original staff/rod/stick to his brother and spokesman Aaron.\f* staff and throw it down in front of Pharaoh so it turns into a snake.”
\v 10 So Moses and Aaron went in to Pharaoh’s \add courtyard\add* and did what Yahweh had told them: Aaron threw his staff down in front of Pharaoh and his servants and it turned into a snake.
\v 11 Then Pharaoh also called for his wise men and sorcerers, and the Egyptian magicians did \add the same thing\add* using their magic—
\v 12 each \add of them\add* threw down his staff and they became snakes, \add except that\add* Aaron’s staff swallowed up their staffs.
\v 13 \add But\add* Pharaoh’s mind was made up and he took no notice \add of them\add* as Yahweh had said \add earlier\add*.
\s1 Plague 1: Water to blood
\p
\v 14 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Pharaoh is very stubborn \add and\add* he’s refused to let the people go.
\v 15 \add So\add* go to Pharaoh. Listen, \add every\add* morning he goes \add down\add* to the \add river\add*, so station yourself to meet him on the bank. Take the staff with you that turned into a snake,
\v 16 and tell him, ‘Yahweh, the god of the Hebrews, \add previously\add* sent me to you to say, “Let my people go \add so\add* they can serve me in the wilderness.” But listen, until now you haven’t \add done it\add*
\v 17 so Yahweh says, “\add Now\add* you will know that I am Yahweh because of this: See, I’m about to use my staff to strike the water in the Nile and it will turn to blood
\v 18 and the fish in the river will die and the river will stink. Then \add your people\add* will \add gag\add* to drink \add the\add* water from the river.” ’
\p
\v 19 “Tell Aaron to take his staff and stretch out his hand over the waters of Egypt: over their streams, their canals, and their pools, and over every reservoir of their water, and \add the water\add* will become blood. Then there’ll be blood in \add the entire country\add*—even in wooden or stone containers \add of water\add*.”
\p
\v 20 \add So\add* And Moses and Aaron did \add exactly\add* what Yahweh had commanded: Aaron raised the staff and struck the river, while Pharaoh and his servants \add watched\add*, and the \add entire\add* river turned to blood.
\v 21 Then the fish in the river died and the river stank, so the Egyptians couldn’t drink water from the river, and the blood was all \add over\add* Egypt.
\v 22 \add But\add* the Egyptian sorcerers did the same with their magic, and \add so\add* Pharaoh stayed stubborn and wouldn’t listen to them, just as Yahweh had said \add earlier\add*.
\v 23 The Pharaoh turned around and went \add back to\add* his house, and he put all of that out of his mind.
\v 24 \add Meanwhile,\add* all the Egyptians dug holes around the river to get water to drink, because river water was undrinkable.
\s1 Plague 2: Frogs everywhere
\p
\v 25 So a week went by since Yahweh had struck the river
\c 8
\nb
\v 1 and he said to Moses, “Go to Pharaoh and tell him, ‘Yahweh says that you \add must\add* let my people go \add so\add* they can serve me.\f + \fr 8:1 \ft This is 7:26 in some versifications (and the following three verses are 7:27, 28, & 29).\f*
\v 2 \add But\add* if you refuse to let \add them\add* go, then look, I’m about to pound your entire territory with frogs.
\v 3 The frogs will swarm in the river, then they’ll climb out and go into your house and in your bedroom and onto your bed. They’ll go into your servants’ houses and among your people, and in your \add cooking\add* bowls and into your ovens.
\v 4 They’ll climb on you and on your people and on all your servants.’ ”
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh said to Moses, “Tell Aaron: Hold your staff out over the canals, the streams, and the pools, and cause the frogs to come up onto Egypt.”
\v 6 So Aaron reached out his hand over the Egyptian water \add supplies\add* and frogs came up and covered Egypt.
\v 7 \add But\add* the sorcerers did the same with their magic, and they \add too\add* brought up frogs over Egypt.
\p
\v 8 Then Pharaoh called for Moses and Aaron, and told \add them\add*, “Ask Yahweh to take the frogs away from me and from my people. Then I’ll let your people go, and they can sacrifice to Yahweh.”
\p
\v 9 Moses answered, “I’ll pray for you and your servants and your people, \add praying that\add* the frogs will keep away from you and your homes and only be left in the river. \add And I’ll give you the honour of specifying when it’ll happen\add*.”
\p
\v 10 “Tomorrow \add morning\add*,” \add the king replied\add*.
\p “Just as you say,” Moses \add responded\add*, “so that you’ll know that there’s no one \add else\add* like our god Yahweh.
\v 11 \add Tomorrow\add* the frogs will leave you and go away from your houses, and from your servants and your people. They’ll only be left in the river.”
\v 12 Then Moses and Aaron left Pharaoh and Moses called out to Yahweh concerning the \add retraction\add* of the frogs that he’d sent against \add the king\add*.
\v 13 So Yahweh did what Moses had requested and the frogs died off from the houses and the courtyards, and from the fields.
\v 14 \add The people\add* collected them into many piles and the \add whole country\add* stank.
\v 15 \add But when\add* Pharaoh saw that there was \add relief from the frogs\add*, he \add firmed his resolve\add* and didn’t listen to Moses and Aaron, just as Yahweh had said \add previously\add*.
\s1 Plague 3: Lice
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh said to Moses, “Tell Aaron: Stretch out your staff and strike the dust on the ground, and it will turn into lice\f + \fr 8:16 \ft The Hebrew word ‘כֵּן/כִּנִּם’ (kēn/kinnim) could refer to either lice, gnats, or fleas. However, unlike lice, gnats don’t usually \+em reside\+em* on people and animals (which seems to be implied by the text).\f* all over Egypt.”
\v 17 So Aaron held out his staff and struck the dust \add with it\add*, and \add the dust\add* became lice on the people and on the animals. All over Egypt, the dust turned into lice
\v 18 and the sorcerers tried to do the same with their magic but they were unable to. \add So\add* there were lice on people and animals
\v 19 and the magicians said to Pharaoh, “This is God’s finger.” \add But Pharaoh\add* was stubborn and he didn’t listen to them, just as Yahweh had said \add previously\add*.
\s1 Plague 4: The swarm
\p
\v 20 Then Yahweh said to Moses, “Get up early in the morning and \add when you see Pharaoh\add* coming down to the river, station yourself in front of \add him\add* and tell him, ‘Yahweh saw that you \add must\add* let my people go \add so\add* they can serve me.
\v 21 Certainly if you don’t release my people \add immediately\add*, then take note: I’m about to send the swarm\f + \fr 8:21 \ft The Hebrew word ‘עָרֹב’ (ˊārov) probably meaning ‘mixture’ is sometimes translated as ‘swarm’. This plague could have been anything from flies to other insects (hornets or mosquitoes???) all the way to a mixture of wild animals.\f* at you and your servants and at your people and into your houses. The Egyptians’ houses will be full of the swarm and even the ground that they’re on.
\v 22 \add But when it happens,\add* I’ll distinguish the Goshen region where my people live so the swarm won’t be there, \add so that you’ll be able to see\add* that I’m Yahweh \add and I’m right here\add* in this land.
\v 23 This sign will happen tomorrow and \add you’ll see how I plan to set my people free\add* from your people.’ ”
\v 24 So Yahweh did \add what he’d said\add* and a heavy swarm came to Pharaoh’s house and the houses of his servants and all the Egyptians—the country was ruined by the \add arrival\add* of the swarm.
\p
\v 25 Then Pharaoh called for Moses and to Aaron and told \add them\add*, “Go and sacrifice to the god of you all, \add but here in Egypt\add*.”
\p
\v 26 “It wouldn’t be right to do that,” Moses replied, “because our sacrifices to Yahweh our god \add would be offensive\add* to Egyptians. Listen, if we made sacrifices in front of the Egyptians \add that are repulsive to them\add*, wouldn’t they \add throw rocks at us to try to kill us\add*?
\v 27 We need to travel into the wilderness for three days, then we’ll sacrifice to our god Yahweh \add there\add*, just like he’s telling us to.”
\p
\v 28 So Pharaoh \add relented\add*, “I’ll let you all go into the wilderness and sacrifice to Yahweh your god—except you definitely can’t go very far. \add Now,\add* ask God for me \add for the plague to stop\add*.”
\p
\v 29 “Listen, I’m about to \add leave now\add*,” Moses responded, “and I’ll pray to Yahweh and he’ll withdraw the swarm from Pharaoh tomorrow, and from his servants and his people. \add But\add* may Pharaoh not be dishonest \add again and\add* fail to let our people go to sacrifice to Yahweh.”
\p
\v 30 Then Moses left Pharaoh and prayed to Yahweh
\v 31 and Yahweh did \add what\add* Moses \add asked him\add* and he withdrew the swarm from Pharaoh, and from his servants and his people. Not one remained.
\v 32 \add But\add* Pharaoh \add changed his mind and\add* remained stubborn \add and he wouldn’t let the Israelites leave\add*.
\c 9
\s1 Plague 5: The stock disease
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh said to Moses, “Go in to Pharaoh and tell him: ‘This is what the god of the Hebrews. Yahweh, says: Let my people go \add so\add* they can serve me.
\v 2 Certainly if you’re unwilling to release them and if you keep holding onto them,
\v 3 then listen. Yahweh is about to \add inflict\add* a very serious deadly disease onto your livestock which are in the fields—on the horses, the donkeys, the camels, the cattle, and on the flock.
\v 4 \add But\add* Yahweh will distinguish between the livestock of the Israelites and the Egyptians—none of the Israelites’ animals will die.’ ”
\v 5 Then Yahweh \add said when it would happen\add*, “Yahweh will do this \add to Egypt\add* tomorrow.”
\p
\v 6 So the next day Yahweh did \add what he’d said\add* and all of the livestock of the Egyptians died, yet none at all from the livestock of the Israelites died.
\v 7 Pharaoh sent \add people to investigate\add*, and wow, not even one of the livestock of the Israelites had died. \add But\add* Pharaoh was strong-willed and wouldn’t let the people go.
\s1 Plague 6: Boils
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron, “Take handfuls of ash out of a furnace, then Moses should \add toss it up in the air in front\add* of Pharaoh.
\v 9 It will become a powder over all Egypt and then it will turn into boils—bursting blisters—on people and on animals all over Egypt.”
\v 10 So they took some ash from a furnace, and standing in front of Pharaoh, Moses \add tossed it up into the air\add* and it turned into boils with bursting blisters on the people and the animals.
\v 11 Because of the boils, the sorcerers weren’t \add even\add* able to stand \add there with\add* Moses because the sorcerers \add suffered with them\add* along with all the Egyptians.
\v 12 \add But\add* Yahweh caused Pharaoh to remain stubborn and he didn’t listen to them, just as Yahweh had told Moses \add previously\add*.
\s1 Plague 7: Hail
\p
\v 13 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Get up early in the morning, and stand in front of Pharaoh and say to him, ‘This is what Yahweh, the god of the Hebrews, says: Let my people go so they can serve me
\v 14 because this time, I’m about to send all my plagues to you and your servants, and your people. \add You’ll all be deeply distressed by them\add*, in order that you might know that there is no \add one else\add* like me in all the world.
\v 15 \add By this time\add*, I could have reached out and struck you and your people with such deadly disease or destruction that you would all have been obliterated from the land.
\v 16 However, I’ve kept you for this reason: to show you my power and in order to declare my name all over the world.
\v 17 You’re still considering yourself to be greater than my people by failing to release them.
\v 18 \add Now\add* listen: I’m about to cause extremely heavy hail to rain \add down\add* at this time tomorrow. \add It’ll be hail like\add* which has never been \add seen before\add* in Egypt from the day it was founded until now.
\v 19 So send for your livestock and everything you own in the country now, and bring it in under cover because the hail will come down on every person and animal that is found in the countryside and not \add sheltering under a roof\add*, and they will die.’ ”
\v 20 Some of Pharaoh’s servants who believed what Yahweh said, quickly brought their slaves and their livestock back into their houses,
\v 21 but those \add people\add* who didn’t believe Yahweh \add just\add* left their slaves and their animals out in the countryside.
\p
\v 22 “Raise your arm up toward the sky,” Yahweh told Moses, “and hail \add will strike\add* all over Egypt—on the people and animals and on every plant in the Egyptian countryside.”
\v 23 So Moses lifted his staff to the sky, and Yahweh sent thunder and hail, and \add lightning caused fires\add* on the land, and \add so\add* Yahweh rained hail \add down\add* onto Egypt.
\v 24 There was hail, and \add also\add* fire coming \add down\add* in the middle of the hail. It was very heavy, such as had never been in all of Egypt since it became a nation.
\v 25 The hail struck everything in Egypt—everything that was in the countryside, from people to animals. \add It also\add* struck every plant and broke every tree \add out\add* in the countryside.
\v 26 Only in the Goshen region where the Israelites were, was there no hail.
\p
\v 27 Then Pharaoh sent for Moses and Aaron and told them, “I’ve sinned this time. Yahweh is righteous, and me and my people are guilty.
\v 28 Pray to Yahweh \add to stop it,\add* because the thunder of God and hail are too much. Then I’ll release you all—you won’t have to remain \add here\add*.”
\p
\v 29 “As I’m leaving the city,” Moses replied, “I’ll spread my palms \add out\add* to Yahweh. The thunder will stop and the hail won’t continue so that you \add will be able to see\add* that the earth belongs to Yahweh.
\v 30 \add But as for\add* you and your servants, I know that you still don’t fear Yahweh God.”
\p
\v 31 (\add Now\add* the flax and the barley were \add flattened by the hail\add* because the barley was \add full grown and starting to fill out\add* and the flax was in bloom.
\v 32 But the wheat and the spelt weren’t \add destroyed because they come later in the season\add*.)
\p
\v 33 So Moses left Pharaoh, and as he was leaving the city, he spread his palms \add out\add* to Yahweh and the thunder and hail ceased, and the rain stopped pouring down on the land.
\v 34 \add But when\add* Pharaoh saw that the rain and hail and thunder had stopped, he added to his sins and he \add firmed his resolve\add*—he and his servants.
\v 35 And \add so\add* Pharaoh remained stubborn and didn’t let the Israelites leave, just as Yahweh had said through Moses \add previously\add*.
\c 10
\s1 Plague 8: Locusts
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Go in to Pharaoh because I’ve made him and his servants stubborn in order to \add be able to\add* demonstrate these miracles of mine among them,
\v 2 and so that \add in the future you’ll be able to tell your descendants\add* that I \add made\add* Egypt \add look foolish\add*, and my miracles which I demonstrated among them—and \add so that\add* you will know that I am Yahweh.”
\p
\v 3 So Moses and Aaron went in to Pharaoh and told him, “This is what Yahweh, God of the Hebrews says: \add how long before you’ll submit to me\add*? Let my people go \add so\add* they can serve me,
\v 4 because if you refuse to release them, then listen, I’ll bring locusts into your territory tomorrow.
\v 5 They’ll cover the ground so that no one will \add even\add* be able to see it, and they’ll devour whatever remains after the hail. Plus they’ll devour every tree growing in the countryside.
\v 6 They’ll fill your houses and the houses of all your servants and the houses of all the Egyptians. It’ll be nothing like anything which your \add parents generation\add* ever saw, nor anything seen by the \add previous generations\add* from the day they came to be on the earth until today.” Then Moses turned around and went out from Pharaoh \add with Aaron\add*.
\p
\v 7 Then Pharaoh’s servants \add asked\add* him, “How much longer is this \add going to cause trouble\add* for us? Let the men go and they’ll worship their god Yahweh. Don’t you understand yet that Egypt \add is being destroyed\add*?”
\p
\v 8 So Moses and Aaron were brought back to Pharaoh, and he told them, “Go and worship your god Yahweh. Who \add are the ones\add* who’ll be going?”
\p
\v 9 “We’ll take our children and our old folks,” Moses answered. “We’ll go with our sons and daughters, and with our flocks and herds, because the festival of Yahweh is for \add all of\add* us.”
\p
\v 10 “\add You’d need Yahweh with you if I ever\add* let you and your children go \add together\add*,” Pharaoh responded. “You’re clearly \add plotting an evil trick\add*!
\v 11 \add Well that won’t happen!\add* The men may go and worship Yahweh since that’s what you’ve been wanting.” Then he drove them \add out of the room\add*.
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Extend your hand over Egypt and let \add the locusts\add* rise up over the land, and let \add them\add* devour all the plants—everything which the hail had spared.”
\v 13 So Moses stretched his staff out over Egypt, and Yahweh sent an east wind over the land all that day and all night. Then morning came, and the east wind had brought the locusts.
\v 14 The locusts covered across all of Egypt and came to rest everywhere within the \add country’s\add* borders. They were extremely numerous—never before were there so many locusts, and it’ll never be \add repeated again\add*.
\v 15 \add The cloud of locusts blocked the sun\add* and \add prevented people from being able to see very far\add*. They devoured all the plants on the land and all of the fruit on the trees that the hail had left, and \add no piece\add* of greenery remained on any tree or plant in the entire Egyptian countryside.
\p
\v 16 Pharaoh quickly summoned Moses and Aaron and said, “I’ve sinned against your god Yahweh and against you.
\v 17 So now just this once, please \add overlook\add* my sin and pray to your god Yahweh and just let him take this \add deathly plague\add* away from me.”
\v 18 So Moses left Pharaoh and went out and prayed to Yahweh,
\v 19 and Yahweh \add caused\add* a very strong sea wind. \add The wind\add* carried away the locusts and drove them into the Red sea—not a single locust remained anywhere in Egyptian territory.
\v 20 \add But\add* Yahweh caused Pharaoh \add to\add* remain stubborn and he wouldn’t release the Israelites.
\s1 Plague 9: Darkness
\p
\v 21 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Raise your hand up toward the sky and let it become dark over all Egypt—darkness \add that can be felt\add*.”
\v 22 So Moses stretched his hand out \add towards\add* the sky, and a \add thick\add* darkness was in all Egypt for three days.
\v 23 No one could see \add anyone else\add*, and for three days no one was able to go anywhere, yet there was light in \add the region where\add* the Israelites lived.
\p
\v 24 Then Pharaoh summoned Moses and told him, “Go serve Yahweh. Your children can go with you all, \add but\add* your flocks and your herds must remain behind.”
\p
\v 25 “On top of that,” Moses responded, “you must also give us the sacrifices and offerings to be burnt when we will \add sacrifice to\add* Yahweh our god.
\v 26 And even our livestock needs to go with us. Not a hoof will be left behind because we’ll \add choose\add* from them to serve Yahweh our god. \add You see\add*, we won’t know what we must \add sacrifice to\add* Yahweh until we arrive there.”
\p
\v 27 \add But\add* Yahweh caused Pharaoh to \add strengthen his resolve\add* and he wouldn’t agree to let them go.
\v 28 “Get \add out of here\add*!” Pharaoh \add shouted\add*. “And watch yourself! You’ll never see me again, because if I ever see you again, I’ll \add have you executed\add*!”
\p
\v 29 “\add Yes,\add*” Moses responded. “It’s just as you say: I’ll never see you again!”
\c 11
\s1 Plague 10: Death of oldest sons
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses, “I’ll send one more plague to Pharaoh and Egypt. After that he’ll let you all go from here. \add What’s more,\add* when he does he’ll actually chase you all right out of here.
\v 2 \add Go and\add* quietly tell the people that both men and women should ask their \add Egyptian\add* neighbours for items of gold and silver.”
\v 3 Then Yahweh gave the Israelites favour in the eyes of the Egyptians. What’s more, Moses was very prestigious in the eyes of Pharaoh’s servants and in the eyes of the Egyptian people.
\p
\v 4 Then Moses told \add Pharaoh\add*, “This is what Yahweh says: Sometime around the middle of the night I’ll go out \add around\add* Egypt,
\v 5 and every oldest son in Egypt will die, from the oldest son of Pharaoh who sits on his throne, to the oldest son of the slave girl who \add lives\add* behind the mill, as well as every oldest male offspring of your livestock.
\v 6 Then there’ll be loud wailing right across Egypt which will be nothing anything that’s ever happened before, and nothing like it will \add ever\add* happen again.
\v 7 But amongst the Israelites, the dogs won’t growl at any person or animals so that you’ll know that Yahweh distinguishes between Egypt and Israel.
\v 8 Then all your servants will come down to me, and they’ll bow to me, pleading, ‘Go, you and all the people who follow you.’ And after that I’ll leave Egypt.” Then Moses left Pharaoh in a fiery rage.
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Pharaoh refused \add to\add* listen to you, so that I’m able to multiply my miracles in Egypt.”
\v 10 Moses and Aaron had done all those miracles in front of Pharaoh \add but\add* Yahweh had made Pharaoh stubborn \add so\add* he hadn’t let the Israelites leave his country.
\c 12
\s1 Meal preparations to avoid death
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron in Egypt,
\v 2 “This month is to become the beginning of the months for you all—\add from now on\add* it will be the first month of the \add new\add* year.
\v 3 Assemble the people and tell them that on the tenth of this month, every man who’s the head of the household must select a lamb or young goat \add for\add* his \add family\add*.
\v 4 \add But\add* if the household’s too small \add to cook\add* a whole animal, then \add neighbours can combine and share—budgeting\add* on how much each person eats.
\v 5 You all can choose either a sheep or a goat, but it must be a perfect, one-year-old male—without any defects.
\v 6 You all must take special care of those animals until the fourteenth day of this month, then every Israelite household will slaughter their animal early that evening.
\v 7 Then they must take some of the blood and put it on both doorposts and on the lintel of the houses that they’ll be in when they eat it.
\v 8 They must roast them on the fire and eat the meat that night, along with flat bread and bitter herbs.
\v 9 Don’t eat any of the meat raw and don’t boil it in water—it must be roasted over the fire with its head, legs, and internal organs.
\v 10 Nothing must be left by morning—anything that’s not eaten must be burnt in the fire.
\v 11 And this is how you all must eat it: with your belts fastened on your waists, your sandals on your feet, and your staffs in your hands. You all must eat it in a hurry. It is the Passover to Yahweh.
\p
\v 12 Then during that night, I will move throughout Egypt and I will kill all the oldest males—both people and animals. I will execute judgement on all the gods of Egypt. I am Yahweh.
\v 13 That blood \add on the doorposts and lintel\add* will be your sign on the houses which you are all in. I will see the blood and will pass over above you all, and the plague won’t \add affect\add* you all with destruction as I strike against Egypt.
\v 14 Then this day will become a memorial for you all, and you’ll celebrate it as a festival to Yahweh for \add future\add* generations—a statute that you’ll all celebrate forever.
\s1 The ‘Flat Bread Celebration’
\p
\v 15 For seven days you all must only eat flat bread \add made without yeast\add*. Yes, on the first day you all must remove any yeast from your houses, because anyone who eats bread with yeast during those seven days, that person must be permanently driven out of Israel.
\v 16 On the first day, you all must gather for a sacred assembly, and similarly on the seventh day. No work can be done on those \add days\add* other than meal preparation.
\v 17 You all must \add regularly\add* observe the \it Flat Bread \+add Celebration\+add*\it*, because on this very day I will have brought your divisions out from Egypt. You all must observe this day throughout \add future\add* generations—a statute that you’ll all celebrate forever.
\v 18 Starting on 14th of the first month in the evening, you all must eat \add only\add* flat bread until the 21st day of the month in the evening.
\v 19 For seven days you all must have no yeast in your homes, because anyone who eats bread with yeast in it, that person \add must be driven out\add* from the Israelite people whether they’re a foreigner or a native.
\v 20 You all mustn’t eat \add anything made with\add* yeast. Wherever you live, you all must only eat flat bread.
\s1 The first ever ‘pass-over’ celebration
\p
\v 21 Then Moses summoned the Israelite elders and told them, “Go ahead and select a lamb or young goat for each family and slaughter it.
\v 22 Then you all must take a bunch of \add leafy\add* hyssop \add stalks\add*, and dip it in the basin \add with the blood from the sacrifice\add* and paint the blood onto the lintel and both doorposts. \add After that\add*, absolutely no one should go out the door of your houses until morning.
\v 23 Then Yahweh will pass through \add out Egypt\add* to strike the Egyptians and \add when\add* he sees the blood on the lintel and on the two doorposts, he’ll ‘pass over’ that doorway and won’t permit ‘the destroyer’ to enter into your houses to strike.
\v 24 All of you and your descendants must maintain this as a law and celebrate it forever,
\v 25 so when you all enter into the land that Yahweh will give to you just as he promised, then you must observe this ceremony.
\v 26 Then when it occurs, your children will ask, ‘What does this ceremony \add mean\add* to you?’
\v 27 you all must tell them, ‘It’s the sacrifice of ‘pass-over’ to Yahweh, who passed over the houses of the Israelites in Egypt \add when he\add* struck the Egyptians and rescued our families.’ ”
\p And the people bowed their heads and prostrated themselves,
\v 28 and the Israelites went and did \add just\add* as Yahweh had commanded \add through\add* Moses and Aaron.
\s1 The death of all the oldest male offspring
\p
\v 29 Then, in the middle of the night, Yahweh struck all the oldest males in Egypt, from the oldest son of Pharaoh who sat on his throne, to the firstborn of the prisoner in the dungeon and all \add oldest male offspring\add* of the animals.
\v 30 During the night, all the Egyptians got up, including Pharaoh and all his servants. There was tremendous wailing throughout Egypt because \add it was a very rare house\add* where there wasn’t someone dead.
\v 31 Pharaoh called for Moses and Aaron in the night and told them, “Pack up and get out from among my people—you \add two\add* and all the other Israelites. Go and serve Yahweh as you’ve said.
\v 32 Also take your flocks and your herds as you’ve said, and go. And bless me as well.
\p
\v 33 The Egyptians urged the Israelites, hurrying to let them go from the land because they said, “We’re all dying.”
\p
\v 34 So the people carried their \add bread\add* dough before it could rise. Their bread-making bowls were wrapped in their clothes on their shoulders.
\p
\v 35 The Israelites did everything that Moses had instructed them, \add including\add* asking for items of gold and silver, and clothing from the Egyptians.
\v 36 Yahweh gave the people favour in the eyes of the Egyptians, \add so by\add* their requests, they were able to strip the Egyptians of their wealth.
\p
\v 37 Then the Israelites walked from Ra’meses toward Sukot—about 600,000 strong men on foot, not counting \add women and\add* children.
\v 38 A large assortment of other people also went with them, along with large numbers of sheep and goats, and cattle.
\p
\v 39 They baked the dough that they brought from Egypt into flat bread since it hadn’t had time to rise,\f + \fr 12:39 \ft Most modern readers probably visualise yeast bought from a supermarket when ‘leaven’ (or even ‘yeast’) is mentioned in a Bible translation. However, it’s much more likely that each day, a portion of the bread dough was set aside for the natural yeasts (think ‘sour dough’ bread) to accumulate, and then mixed into the new batch of dough the next day and left for the natural yeasts to work and rise the dough. The text here implies that it’s possible that the Hebrew word translated ‘unleavened’ really meant ‘unrisen’ on that first ‘pass-over’ night.\f* because they were driven out of Egypt in a hurry and hadn’t had time to prepare food to take.
\p
\v 40 The descendants of Israel \add also called Jacob\add* \add )\add* had lived in Egypt for 430 years
\v 41 to the day, because that very day was when all the \add various\add* divisions of Yahweh’s \add people\add* left Egypt.
\v 42 It was a \add time\add* when they stayed awake all night as Yahweh took them out of Egypt—a night to be observed in that same way by every future generation.
\s1 Instructions for observing the ‘pass-over’
\p
\v 43 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron, “This is the statute of the ‘pass-over’: No foreigners may \add share in\add* the meal \add that night\add*,
\v 44 unless it’s a slave that you bought and who’s been circumcised—
\v 45 no foreigner or hired helper can \add join in\add* the meal.
\v 46 \add Each animal\add* must be eaten in only one house—don’t take any of the meat outside. And don’t break any of the animals bones.
\v 47 Every Israelite must observe it,
\v 48 and if someone is living among you and wants to join in Yahweh’s ‘pass-over’, then every male in their family must be circumcised. Only then will they be allowed to observe it, and then they should be considered like a native of the land. No uncircumcised person may eat it.
\v 49 This one law will apply to both the natives, and those who live among you all.”
\v 50 All the Israelites followed those instructions that Yahweh had given through Moses and Aaron,
\v 51 and \add so\add* on that very day, Yahweh took all the Israelites out of Egypt \add grouped\add* by their \add family\add* divisions.
\c 13
\s1 God owns the first child
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “All firstborn \add offspring\add* are to be consecrated to me—the first product of every womb, whether human or animal, belongs to me.”
\s1 The ‘Flat Bread Celebration’
\p
\v 3 Then Moses told the people, “\add Always\add* remember this day, that you went out of Egypt, \add rescued\add* from slavery, because Yahweh used his power to bring you out from there. And nothing with yeast in it can be eaten \add when you celebrate this\add*.
\v 4 Today you are leaving in the month of Aviv,
\v 5 and \add when\add* Yahweh brings you into the area occupied by the Canaanites, Hittites, Amorites, Hivim, and the Jebusites (which he swore to your fathers to give to you—a very fertile land) you must observe this ceremony in this \add same\add* month.
\v 6 You must eat \add only\add* flat bread for seven days, and then on the seventh day, there will be a festival to \add honour\add* Yahweh.
\v 7 \add Again,\add* you must only eat flat bread for seven days—\add not only that\add* you mustn’t \add even have\add* any risen bread, nor must you have any yeast within any of your borders.
\v 8 On that \add festival\add* day, you must tell your \add children\add*, ‘This is because of what Yahweh did for \add us\add* when \add we left\add* Egypt.’
\v 9 It’ll be like a reminder written on your hand and hanging from your forehead, so that you’ll remember to pass on Yahweh’s instructions because he used his power to bring you out of Egypt.
\v 10 So you must follow those instructions at the appointed time year after year.
\s1 Offering up the oldest children
\p
\v 11 Later, when Yahweh brings you into the land of the Canaanites as he promised to you and your ancestors, and he gives it to you,
\v 12 then hand over to Yahweh all \add your\add* oldest sons, and all the oldest, male animal offspring that are yours. They will \add then\add* belong to Yahweh.
\v 13 In the case of the oldest donkey foal, you must buy it back \add by killing a lamb\add* instead of it. If you don’t buy it back, you must kill the donkey by breaking its neck. You \em must\em* buy back every one of your oldest sons,
\v 14 and in the future when your son asks you, ‘What’s that about?’ then you must tell him, ‘We were enslaved in Egypt , but Yahweh used his incredible power to bring us out.
\v 15 And so it was that when Pharaoh was too stubborn to let us go, then Yahweh struck all of the oldest sons and oldest male animals in Egypt. That’s why \add we\add* offer all the oldest offspring to Yahweh, and why \add we need to\add* buy back all our oldest sons.’
\v 16 So you must place a reminder on your hand and hanging on your forehead that Yahweh used his incredible power to bring us out of Egypt.”
\s1 Led by cloud and fire
\p
\v 17 When Pharaoh released the people, God didn’t lead them through the area occupied by the Philistines, although that way was shorter, because he said, “It’s best if the people don’t have to face war immediately, or they might change their minds and \add then want to\add* return to Egypt.”
\v 18 So God caused the people to take the path going towards the wilderness and the Red sea.\f + \fr 13:18 \ft Probably towards the \+em Gulf of Suez\+em*: see https://armstronginstitute.org/339-where-did-the-red-sea-crossing-take-place.\f* The Israelite left Egypt in formation as if ready for battle.
\p
\v 19 Moses \add ensured that\add* Joseph’s bones were taken with \add them\add*, because \add many years back\add* Joseph had made the Israelites promise, saying, “God will certainly take notice of you all, and you must take my bones out from here with you.”
\p
\v 20 The people travelled on from Sukot and camped at Etam on the edge of the wilderness.
\v 21 During the day, Yahweh went ahead of them in a pillar of cloud to lead them on the way, and at nighttime, he went in a pillar of fire to be light to them, \add so they could travel both\add* in the daytime and at night.
\v 22 The daytime pillar of cloud and the nighttime pillar of fire, didn’t ever leave their place ahead of the people.
\c 14
\s1 Crossing the Red sea
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Tell the Israelites that they should turn and camp in front of Pi-Hahirot, between Migdol and the sea, in front of Baal-Tsefon. You all are to camp by the sea opposite it.
\v 3 Then Pharaoh will say, ‘They are confused \add and lost\add*—the wilderness has boxed them in.’
\v 4 I’ll strengthen Pharaoh’s resolve and he’ll chase after them. Then because of Pharaoh and all his army, I’ll be \add honoured\add* and the Egyptians will know that I am Yahweh.” So the Israelites camped where they were instructed.
\p
\v 5 When the king of Egypt was told that the Israelites had fled, he and his servants \add started thinking about their own\add* people and they asked, “What have we done by letting the Israelite slaves go free?”
\v 6 Then he hitched up his chariot and took his \add army\add* with him.
\v 7 He took 600 of the best chariots as well as all the other Egyptian chariots and officers \add to supervise\add* them.
\v 8 Yahweh strengthened the Egyptian king’s resolve and so Pharaoh pursued after the Israelites after they had confidently marched out \add of Egypt\add*.
\v 9 The Egyptians pursued them, then all of Pharaoh’s horses and chariots, along with his mounted soldiers and his foot army, overtook them camping above the sea above Pi-Hahirot, across from Baal-Tsefon.
\p
\v 10 As \add the Egyptians\add* approached, the Israelites looked back, and \add to their horror\add*, saw the army coming after them. They were extremely afraid and cried out to Yahweh.
\v 11 They \add complained\add* to Moses, “Is it because there weren’t any graves in Egypt that you brought us here to die in the wilderness? What have you done to us? Why did you bring us out from Egypt?
\v 12 Isn’t that \add exactly what we told\add* you in Egypt when we said, ‘Just leave us alone and let us serve the Egyptians’? Because serving the Egyptians \add sounds a lot\add* better to us than dying \add out here\add* in the wilderness.”
\p
\v 13 Then Moses told the people, “You mustn’t be afraid. Stand still and see how Yahweh saves you all—he’s going to do it today, because any Egyptians that you see today, you’ll never, ever see them again.
\v 14 Yahweh himself will fight for you all—all you need to do is to remain calm.”
\v 15 Then Yahweh \add scolded\add* Moses, “Why are you \add still here\add* crying out to me? Talk to the Israelites and get them moving forward.
\v 16 As for you, raise your staff and stretch your arm out over the sea and divide it. Then the Israelites will go on onto dry ground through the middle of the sea.
\v 17 \add Yes, watch me!\add* I’ll strengthen the resolve of the Egyptians and they’ll enter \add between the sea\add* after them. Then I’ll be honoured because of \add what will happen to\add* Pharaoh and all his army, and his chariots and his horsemen.
\v 18 The honour that I gain through that will cause the Egyptians to know that I’m Yahweh.”
\p
\v 19 Then God’s messenger who travelled ahead of the Israelites, moved and went behind them. \add Also\add* the pillar of cloud moved from in front of them and stood behind them
\v 20 and \add so\add* it was then between the \add coming\add* Egyptians and the Israelites. Then the cloud became darkness \add on the Egyptian\add* \add side\add*, \add yet it lit up the other side\add*, and no one could approach it all night.
\v 21 Then Moses stretched his arm out over the sea and Yahweh sent a strong east wind. It blew all night and divided the sea \add on each side\add* and dried the \add strip of\add* land in the middle.
\v 22 So then the Israelites entered through the middle of the sea on dry ground with a wall of water on each side of them.
\v 23 \add However,\add* the Egyptians pursued after them. Every one of Pharaoh’s horses and chariots and his horsemen \add also\add* entered into the middle of the sea.
\v 24 \add By now morning was coming\add* and Yahweh looked down through the pillar of fire and cloud, and he caused confusion for the Egyptian army.
\v 25 He caused the wheels of their chariots to turn unevenly \add so they became difficult\add* to drive, and the Egyptians \add complained\add*, “Let’s retreat from \add following\add* the Israelites, because Yahweh is fighting against Egypt and for them.”
\p
\v 26 \add On the other side,\add* Yahweh told Moses, “Stretch your arm out over the sea and the waters will \add flow back\add* onto the Egyptians and onto Pharaoh’s chariots and his horsemen.”
\v 27 So Moses stretched his arm out over the sea, and as the day broke, the sea began to return to its normal place. The Egyptians \add turned and\add* fled before its impact \add but\add* Yahweh shook the Egyptians \add off their horses and chariots\add* in the middle of the sea.
\v 28 So the sea returned and covered the chariots and the horsemen—even Pharaoh’s entire army that had entered into the sea after the Israelites. Not even one of the pursuers remained.
\v 29 \add But\add* the Israelites had walked through the middle of the sea on dry ground with a wall of water on each side of them.
\p
\v 30 So Yahweh saved Israel from Egypt’s power that day, and the Israelites saw the Egyptians bodies \add washed up\add* on the shore.
\v 31 \add All\add* the Israelites saw the incredible power that Yahweh had used against the Egyptians, and the people revered Yahweh, and put their trust in him and his servant Moses.
\c 15
\s1 Moses’s song
\p
\v 1 Then Moses and the Israelites sang this song to Yahweh:
\q1 “I will sing to Yahweh because he’s been so successful—
\q1 he’s thrown both the horses and their riders into the sea.
\q1
\v 2 I will sing about Yahweh, my source of strength,
\q2 and the one who saves me.
\q1 I will praise him because he’s my god—
\q1 he’s my father’s god and I will honour him.
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh is a warrior.
\q1 Yahweh is his name.
\b
\q1
\v 4 He threw Pharaoh’s chariots and his army into the sea.
\q1 His hand-picked officers have been sunk in the sea.
\b
\q1
\v 5 The deep waters cover them.
\q1 They descended into the depths like a stone.
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh’s right arm is so powerful.
\q1 Yahweh’s right arm shattered our enemy.
\q1
\v 7 You toss away your adversaries with your great power.
\q1 Your burning anger consumes them like straw \add in a fire\add*.
\q1
\v 8 It was your breath that made the waters pile up.
\q1 They stood up like a heap.
\q1 The deep water seemed like it was solid.
\q1
\v 9 Our enemies thought that they could chase after us and overtake us.
\q1 They had planned to satisfy themselves by sharing out the plunder.
\q1 They were ready to use their swords and swing their arms to destroy us.
\q1
\v 10 \add But\add* you blew with your breath.
\q1 The sea covered them.
\q1 They sank like lead in that terrible surge.
\b
\q1
\v 11 Which other god is like you, Yahweh?
\q1 Who else is so powerful and yet sinless,
\q2 receiving praises for doing miracles?
\q1
\v 12 You stretched out your powerful arm.
\q1 The earth swallowed them up.
\q1
\v 13 You are leading the people you bought back,
\q2 because you committed yourself to love them.
\q1 You used your strength to lead them to your sinless home.
\q1
\v 14 Other countries have heard, and they tremble.
\q1 Anguish has struck the inhabitants of Philistia.
\q1
\v 15 The chiefs of Edom have panicked.
\q1 Trembling has seized the leaders of Moab.
\q1 The inhabitants of Canaan have melted away.
\q1
\v 16 Terror and dread have fallen on them.
\q1 \add After seeing\add* your great power, they’ve stayed as still as stone
\q2 until your people have all marched past, Yahweh.
\q1 Until the people you bought have all gone past \add them\add*.
\q1
\v 17 Bring them into \add the land\add*
\q2 and establish them on the mountain of your inheritance.
\q1 The place you’ve chosen to become your home, Yahweh—
\q2 a sanctuary built by your hands, master.
\q1
\v 18 Yahweh will reign \add as king\add* forever and ever.
\q1
\v 19 Pharaoh’s horse went into the sea, along with his chariots and horsemen,\f + \fr 15:19 \ft Some interpreters include v19 as part of Moses’s song (as we do, although it does seem less poetic in structure), while others don’t. (There’s no speech marks in the ancient Hebrew texts.)\f*
\q1 and Yahweh brought the water back over them,
\q1 \add after\add* the Israelites had walked through on dry ground in the middle of the sea.”
\s1 Miriam’s song
\p
\v 20 Then Aaron’s sister Miriam who was a prophetess, picked up a tambourine and went outside. All the other women did likewise and followed her out to dance together
\v 21 so Miriam sang this response:
\q1 “Sing to Yahweh and tell of his amazing victory—
\q1 He tossed the horses and their riders into the sea.”
\s1 The bitter water
\p
\v 22 Then Moses lead the Israelites away from the Red sea and they moved into the wilderness at Shur. They went through the wilderness for three days and \add finally\add* found some water,
\v 23 but it was so bitter that they couldn’t drink it. So they named that place ‘Marah’ \add (which means ‘bitter’)\add*,
\v 24 and the people grumbled about Moses saying, “\add So\add* what will we drink \add then\add*?”
\v 25 So Moses cried out to Yahweh, and Yahweh showed him a tree. When he threw it into the water, the water became \add safe to drink\add*.
\p Yahweh made a statue at that place, and a rule, and he tested Moses there,
\v 26 saying, “If you carefully listen to the voice of Yahweh your god, and you do what’s right in my eyes, and you listen to my commands and you keep all of my laws, then I won’t inflict you with any of the diseases that I put on the Egyptians, because I’m Yahweh your healer.”
\p
\v 27 \add Then they moved on\add* and came to Eylim where there were twelve springs and seventy palm trees, and they camped there by the water.
\c 16
\s1 The manna and the quail
\p
\v 1 Then they left Eylim, and all the Israelites entered into the wilderness at Sin (between Eylim and Sinai) around six weeks after leaving Egypt,
\v 2 and all the Israelites murmured against Moses and Aaron \add there\add* in the wilderness,
\v 3 saying, “If only Yahweh had killed us in Egypt, \add where we’d be\add* sitting by a pot of meat eating bread until we’re full, because you’ve brought us out into this wilderness to starve us all to death.”
\p
\v 4 Then Yahweh told Moses, “\add Just\add* watch me make food rain down from the sky for you all. Then the people will be able to go out every day and gather enough for that day so that I can test them: Will they obey my law or not?
\v 5 But every Friday they can \add gather and\add* prepare twice as much as what they gather on the other days.”
\p
\v 6 So Moses and Aaron told all the Israelites, “This evening you’ll all know that Yahweh was the one who brought you out from Egypt.
\v 7 In the morning you’ll all see Yahweh’s greatness as he’s heard your murmurings against him. \add I mean,\add* who are we that you’d murmur against \em us\em*?”
\v 8 Then Moses continued, “So Yahweh will give you all meat to eat in the evening and enough bread in the morning to make you all full. \add Yes,\add* Yahweh has heard your murmurings against him. As for us, we’re insignificant. Your murmuring isn’t against us, but against Yahweh.”
\p
\v 9 Then Moses told Aaron, “Summon everyone to come and face Yahweh, because he’s heard their murmurings.”
\v 10 And so while Aaron spoke to all the Israelites, they turned toward the wilderness, and wow, Yahweh’s \add dazzling brightness\add* appeared in the cloud!
\v 11 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 12 “I’ve heard the murmurings of the Israelites. Speak to them saying, ‘In the evenings, you’ll all eat meat, and in the mornings you’ll all be satisfied with bread. Then you’ll all know that I’m your god, Yahweh.’ ”
\p
\v 13 And so that evening, quail appeared and covered the camp. And in the morning, \add something like\add* dew was lying all around the camp,
\v 14 and when it evaporated, wow, it left behind thin flakes—thin like frost on the ground there in the wilderness.
\v 15 \add When\add* the Israelites saw it, they asked each other, ‘What is it?’ because they didn’t know what it was.
\p And Moses told them, “It’s the food that Yahweh has given you all to eat.
\v 16 These are Yahweh’s instructions: Gather enough of it according to how many people you’re feeding—about two litres per person who lives in your tents.”
\p
\v 17 So the Israelites went ahead and gathered it—some collecting a lot, and others, less,
\v 18 \add but\add* when they measured it out, those who’d collected a lot didn’t have any extra, and those who hadn’t gathered so much still had enough to eat—each person had gathered just enough.
\v 19 Then Moses told them, “Don’t save any of it for tomorrow,”
\v 20 \add but some of\add* them didn’t take any notice of him and kept it until the next morning. However, by then it was full of maggots and smelt rotten, and Moses was very angry at them.
\p
\v 21 So \add from that day onwards,\add* they gathered it every morning, depending on how many they were feeding, and then when the sun got hot, the rest melted \add away\add*.
\v 22 On the Friday, they gathered twice as much—around \add four litres\add* for each person—and all the leaders of the community came and informed Moses
\v 23 and he told them, “This is what Yahweh said: Tomorrow is a rest day to be set aside for Yahweh. So bake or cook your food, and keep the left-overs overnight to use in the morning.”
\v 24 So they did what Moses had told them and kept the left-overs until the morning, and it didn’t stink or have any bugs in it.
\v 25 On that Saturday morning, Moses told them, “Go ahead and eat the previously cooked food because today is set aside for Yahweh and so you all won’t find any more \add on the ground\add*—
\v 26 you’ll gather it for six days \add each week\add*, but on the Saturday, there won’t be any.”
\p
\v 27 \add However,\add* some people did go out to collect it, but they couldn’t find any,
\v 28 and Yahweh said to Moses, “For how long will you all keep refusing to obey my commands and my laws?
\v 29 Listen, Yahweh has given you the Rest Day, so on the Friday he gives you food for two days. On the Saturday, everyone should stay where they are—there’s no need for anyone to go outside \add searching\add*.”
\v 30 So the people didn’t work on the Saturday.
\p
\v 31 The Israelites named it ‘manna’ \add (which sounds like Hebrew ‘What is it?’)\add*. It was white like coriander seed, and it tasted like wafers with honey.
\v 32 Then Moses said, “This is the procedure that Yahweh has commanded: Keep a full measure of it for preservation for your descendants so that they can see the food which I fed you in the wilderness, when I brought you out from Egypt.”
\v 33 So Moses said to Aaron, “Take a jar and fill it with the full two litres of manna. Place it in front of Yahweh for preservation for your descendants.”
\v 34 \add So\add* Aaron \add prepared the jar and kept it\add* (and would \add eventually\add* place it \add in the box\add* with the \add stone tablets\add*.)
\v 35 The Israelites ate manna for forty years, until they \add eventually\add* left the wilderness and came to the borders of the Canaan region.
\v 36 (Two litres is called an ‘omer’ which is a tenth of an ‘efah’.)
\c 17
\s1 Yahweh gives water from a rock
\r (Num. 20:1-13)
\p
\v 1 Then all the Israelites moved on from Sin in the wilderness, travelling following Yahweh’s instructions. They camped at Refidim \add but\add* there was no water for the people to drink.
\v 2 So the people argued with Moses \add again\add*, saying, “Give us water to drink!”
\p “Why are you all quarrelling with me?” Moses answered. “Why are you all testing Yahweh?”
\p
\v 3 \add But\add* the people were \add very\add* thirsty there and they murmured against Moses, \add so\add* he \add demanded\add*, “Is this why you brought us out of Egypt? To kill me and my sons and my cattle with thirst?” ???
\p
\v 4 Moses cried out to Yahweh, \add asking\add*, “What should I do for these people? It won’t take much more and they’ll kill me.”
\p
\v 5 “Walk on ahead of the people,” Yahweh told Moses, “and take some of the Israelite elders with you, and your staff—the one which you struck the river with. Take it in your hand and go.
\v 6 Watch me. I will stand in front of you there on the rock at Horeb. Then you will strike the rock and water will come out of it, and the people will drink.” \add So\add* Moses did that while the Israelite elders watched.
\p
\v 7 Moses named that place \add both\add* ‘Masah’ \add (which means ‘testing’)\add* and ‘Merivah’ \add (which means ‘complaining’)\add*, because of the conflict among the Israelites and because of how they tested Yahweh by saying, ‘Is Yahweh among us or not?’
\s1 Moses’s arms held high to defeat Amalek
\p
\v 8 Then the Amalek \add army\add* came and attacked Israel at Refidim,
\v 9 and Moses instructed Joshua, “Choose men for us and go out and fight with Amalek. Tomorrow I’ll be stationed on top of the hill with the staff of God in my hand.”
\v 10 So Joshua \add prepared\add* to fight Amalek as Moses had told him, and Moses, Aaron, and Hur climbed up to the top of the hill.
\v 11 And so it happened that when Moses raised his arm, then Israel would be winning, \add but\add* when he lowered his arm, then Amalek would start winning.
\v 12 \add However,\add* Moses’s arms got tired, so they got a \add large\add* stone and sat him on it, and Aaron and Hur stood on each side of him holding up his arms, and holding him steady in that position until the sun went down.
\v 13 So \add that was how\add* Joshua defeated Amalek and his army with their swords.
\v 14 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Write this event in the book and read it aloud to Joshua, because I will certainly blot out the memory of Amalek from off the earth.”
\v 15 Then Moses built an altar and he named it ‘Yahweh-nissi’ \add (which means ‘Yahweh is my banner’)\add*,
\v 16 saying, “Because they battled against Yah’s throne, then Yahweh will battle against Amalek throughout the generations.”
\c 18
\s1 Moses’s father-in-law visits
\p
\v 1 \add Now\add* Jethro, the priest at Midian and Moses’ father-in-law, heard about everything that God had done for Moses and for his people the Israelites—that Yahweh had brought the Israelites out of Egypt.
\v 2 (Jethro had \add been looking after\add* Moses’s wife Zipporah \add and their two sons\add* after he had sent her back
\v 3 along with their two sons. One was named ‘Gereshom’ \add (which sounds like the Hebrew word for ‘foreigner’)\add* because Moses had said, “I’ve been a foreigner living in another country.”
\v 4 \add Their\add* other son’s name was ‘Eliezer’ \add (which sounds like the Hebrew word that means ‘God helps me’\add*, because Moses had said, “God, who my father worshipped, has helped me and stopped the Egyptian king Pharaoh from killing me.”))
\p
\v 5 So \add now\add* Moses’s father-in-law brought Moses’s wife and sons to the wilderness where the people were camping \add near\add* the mountain of God.
\v 6 He had sent a message ahead saying, “I, your father-in-law Jethro, am coming to you, as well as your wife and your two sons.”
\v 7 So Moses went out to meet his father-in-law, and he bowed down and kissed him. They asked each other about their welfare, then they went into the tent,
\v 8 and Moses related to his father-in-law all that Yahweh had done to Pharaoh and to Egypt on account of Israel, all the hardship that found them along the way, and about how Yahweh had rescued them.
\v 9 Jethro was very happy to hear about all the good \add things\add* that Yahweh had done for Israel when he had rescued them out from Egyptian control,
\v 10 and he said, “Bless Yahweh, who rescued you from \add slavery\add* in Egypt, and from Pharaoh’s power.
\v 11 Now I can see that Yahweh is greater than all the \add other\add* gods because of \add this result after\add* the Egyptians had acted proudly against you all.”
\v 12 Then Moses’s father-in-law Jethro made a burnt offering as well as sacrifices to God, and Aaron and all of the Israelite elders came to eat bread with Moses’s father-in-law in God’s presence.
\s1 Jethro’s helpful administrative advice
\r (Deu. 1:9-18)
\p
\v 13 The following day, Moses sat down to judge the people, and the people stood around him from morning until evening.
\v 14 When his father-in-law noticed everything that he \add personally\add* did for the people, he \add asked\add*, “What’s this that you’re doing with the people? Why do you sit \add there\add* alone, and all the people position themselves around you from morning until evening?”
\p
\v 15 “Because the people come to me to find out what God wants for them,” Moses replied to Jethro.
\v 16 “When a \add difficult\add* situation comes up among them, someone comes to me and I make the judgement between a man and his neighbour. \add In addition,\add* I help them understand the god’s statutes and laws.”
\p
\v 17 “It’s not a good \add idea\add* what you’re doing,” said Jethro.
\v 18 “You’re definitely \add on the road to\add* wear yourself out, plus you’ll also wear out these people who are with you, because the \add responsibility\add* is too heavy for you—you can’t \add just\add* do it alone.
\v 19 Now, listen to \add this\add*: I’ll advise you and God will be with you. You be before God for the people, and you should bring their disputes to God yourself.
\v 20 You should \add also\add* teach them the statutes and the laws, and you should \add teach\add* them the way they should \add live\add* and the work that they should do.
\v 21 \add But\add* you yourself should search through all the people to find capable men who are godly and faithful, and not \add greedy for\add* dishonest profit. Then appoint \add the best of them\add* to be over a thousand households, and then others to be over groups of one hundred, fifty, and ten households.
\v 22 They’ll \add be the ones to\add* judge the people, except that every serious situation they’ll bring to you—they’ll judge every minor situation themselves. \add Yes,\add* you must lighten this load off yourself, and they’ll share the load with you.
\v 23 If you do that, and if God tells you \add to proceed\add*, then you’ll be able to endure \add in the long term\add*, plus all of these people \add standing around\add* will be able to return to their tents peacefully.”
\p
\v 24 So Moses took his father-in-law’s advice and implemented all of his suggestions.
\v 25 He chose capable men from among all the Israelites, and he appointed them as heads over the people: leaders of thousands, leaders of hundreds, leaders of fifties, and leaders of tens \add of households\add*.
\v 26 They became the ones who settled most disputes for the people. They brought the difficult cases to Moses, \add but\add* they judged each minor situation themselves.
\p
\v 27 Then Moses bid farewell to Jethro and he returned to his own home.
\c 19
\s1 Camping by Mt. Sinai
\p
\v 1 The Israelites entered the Sinai wilderness in the third month after exiting Egypt.
\v 2 \add After\add* they had departed from Refidim, they had entered the Sinai wilderness and camped there at the base of the mountain.
\v 3 Yahweh called Moses from the mountain, so he climbed up and God gave him this message and asked him to pass it on to Jacob’s descendants, the Israelites:
\p
\v 4 “You yourselves saw what I did to the Egyptians—how I carried you on eagles’ wings and brought you all \add here\add* to myself.
\v 5 And now, if you all carefully listen to my words and keep my agreement, then your nation will be my possession, \add separate\add* from all the \add others\add*, because all the earth is mine,
\v 6 So you all will be my kingdom of priests and a nation \add dedicated to me\add*.” (That’s the message to pass on to the Israelites.)
\v 7 So Moses descended \add again\add* and summoned the Israelite elders, then he passed on that message \add just\add* as Yahweh had commanded him,
\v 8 and all the people responded together, \add agreeing\add*, “We’ll do everything that Yahweh has said.” Then Moses took the people’s response back to Yahweh
\v 9 and Yahweh told him, “Look, I’ll come to you in a thick cloud so that the people will be able to hear when I speak with you \add so they’ll also continue to\add* trust you.”
\p Then Moses told Yahweh the people’s words
\v 10 and Yahweh responded, “Go \add down\add* to the people and purify them today and tomorrow. Have them wash their clothes
\v 11 and be prepared for the third day, because on the third day I will come down to Mt. Sinai while all the people watch.
\v 12 Set boundaries for the people all around, and tell them, ‘Don’t climb on the mountain or touch its edge, because anyone who does will definitely be executed.
\v 13 Anyone \add who touches the mountain\add* mustn’t be touched by anyone else—they must be executed by throwing rocks at them or shooting them \add with a bow\add*. No person or animal \add who touches the mountain\add* can stay alive.’ Only after a long trumpet blast should they \add start to\add* climb the mountain.”
\v 14 So Moses went down the mountain to the people. He purified them and they \add all\add* washed their clothes,
\v 15 and he told them, “Abstain from sexual relations, and be ready by the third day.”
\p
\v 16 So on the morning of the third day, there was thunder and lightning and a thick cloud on the mountain, and an extremely loud horn blast, and all the people in the camp were trembling.
\v 17 Then Moses led the people out of the camp to \add go and\add* meet God, and they stationed themselves at the foot of the mountain.
\v 18 Yahweh descended onto Mt. Sinai \add surrounded\add* by fire and the entire mountain smoked. Its smoke went up like the smoke from a furnace, and the whole mountain trembled violently.
\v 19 Then it happened \add after\add* the sound of the horn got extremely loud, that Moses was speaking and God was answering him in a voice.
\v 20 Yahweh had descended onto the top of Mt. Sinai, and he called Moses up to the top of the mountain, \add so\add* Moses ascended.
\v 21 \add But\add* Yahweh told him, “Go \add back\add* down and warn the people in case they force their way through to look at me and many of them perish.
\v 22 Also, the priests who come nearer to me, they must be very careful to purify themselves in case I suddenly punish them.”
\v 23 \add But\add* Moses \add questioned\add* Yahweh, “The people aren’t able to come up on Mt. Sinai, because you yourself warned us, ‘Set boundaries all around the mountain to keep it sacred.’ ”
\v 24 “Go \add back\add* down,” Yahweh responded, “and bring Aaron back up with you. \add However,\add* the priests and the people must not cross the boundary to come up to me, in case I suddenly punish them.”
\v 25 So Moses went down and spoke to the people.
\c 20
\s1 The ten rules
\r (Deu. 5:1-22)
\p
\v 1 Then God gave these instructions \add to the Israelites\add*:
\v 2 I’m your god Yahweh, who rescued you\f + \fr 20:2 \ft Although modern English doesn’t easily distinguish it, this discourse is addressed to singular ‘you’, i.e., to the Israelites as one group. While some of our modern, individualistic cultures might naturally interpret these rules as applying to individuals, they were given to Israel to implement at a national level.\f* from where you were enslaved in Egypt.
\li1
\v 3 You mustn’t own any other gods in my sight.
\li1
\v 4 You mustn’t make a carved figure for yourself, nor \add carve a copy\add* of anything in the sky above or in the earth below, or that’s in the water under the earth.
\v 5 Don’t bow down to idols and don’t serve them, because I, your god Yahweh, am a jealous God. I remember the sins of the fathers and \add punish\add* the children of \add even\add* the third and fourth \add generation\add* of those who hate me,
\v 6 \add but\add* I faithfully fulfil my promises to \em thousands\em* \add of generations\add* of those who love me and who obey my instructions.
\li1
\v 7 You mustn’t wrongly promote the name of Yahweh your god, because I won’t leave anyone unpunished who does that.
\li1
\v 8 Keep Saturday different from all the other days—
\v 9 there’s six days \add every week\add* for working.
\v 10 The seventh day is a rest day for Yahweh your god: you mustn’t do any work—not you, or your children, or your male or female servants, or your cattle, or the foreigners living among you—
\v 11 because Yahweh made the heavens and earth, the sea, and everything that’s in them in six days. Then he rested on the seventh day, so that’s why he blessed the rest day and made it sacred.
\li1
\v 12 Honour your father and your mother, so that you’ll have a long life on the land that your god Yahweh is about to give you.
\li1
\v 13 You mustn’t murder.
\li1
\v 14 You mustn’t commit adultery.
\li1
\v 15 You mustn’t steal.
\li1
\v 16 You mustn’t lie in court.
\li1
\v 17 You mustn’t covet your neighbour’s house or spouse, or their male or female servants, or their animals, or anything else they own.
\s1 The people are afraid of God
\r (Deu. 5:22-33)
\p
\v 18 All of the people could hear and see the thunder and the lightning, the sound of the horn, and the mountain smoking. They trembled \add when\add* they saw it, and they \add remained\add* standing at a distance.
\v 19 They \add begged\add* Moses, “You be the one to speak to us, and we’ll listen, but don’t let God speak \add directly\add* to us, in case we die.”
\p
\v 20 “Don’t be afraid,” Moses responded, “because God has come to test you so that the fear of him will remain with you so that you all won’t disobey him.”
\v 21 So the people stood at a good distance \add while\add* Moses approached toward the thick darkness where God was.
\s1 Instructions about altars
\p
\v 22 Then Yahweh told Moses, “This is what you need to tell the Israelites: ‘You yourselves have seen that I, \add Yahweh,\add* have talked with you from heaven.
\v 23 You all mustn’t make gods out of silver or gold for yourselves to have alongside me.
\v 24 You need to make an altar for me out of dirt, and then you can sacrifice your burnt offerings and peace offerings, and your sheep and cattle, on it. I’ll inform you about every place where I want to be worshipped and I’ll come to you and will bless you \add there\add*.
\v 25 If you do make me an altar with stones, you mustn’t use cut stones to build it because moving your tools over it will defile it.
\v 26 And don’t use steps to get up to the altar because you might expose your private parts \add under your robes\add*.
\c 21
\s1 Rules about slaves
\r (Deu. 15:12-18)
\p
\v 1 These are the rules that you must present to them:
\s2 Hebrew slaves
\p
\v 2 If you buy a Hebrew slave, he should serve for six years, then in the seventh year he must be set free without having to pay you anything.
\v 3 If he came by himself, he can go free by himself. If he’s married with a wife, then his wife must be set free with him.
\v 4 If his master gave him a wife and she bore him sons or daughters, then the wife and her children are her master’s, and only the husband must be set free.
\v 5 \add But\add* if he plainly says, “I love my master, my wife, and my children so I \add voluntarily\add* choose not to be set free,”
\v 6 then his master must take him to the place of worship. He must bring him to a door or to a doorpost, and his master must bore a hole his ear with an awl. Then he must serve him \add for life\add*.
\p
\v 7 And if a man sells his daughter as a female slave, she won’t be sent away \add after six years\add* like the male slaves.
\v 8 If the man who bought her wanted her to become his slave-wife, but \add later\add* he isn’t pleased with her behaviour, he must allow her to be bought back by her relatives. He has no authority to sell her on to outsiders, because that would break the understanding of proper treatment of her.
\v 9 Or if he selected her to become his son’s slave-wife, then he must treat her like a daughter would expect to be treated.
\v 10 If he go on to take another slave-wife, he mustn’t reduce the first one’s food or clothing, or her marital rights,
\v 11 \add but\add* if he doesn’t provide these three things to her, then she can go free without having to pay anything.
\s1 Rules about violence to others
\p
\v 12 Anyone who strikes someone and that person dies, must definitely be put to death.
\v 13 But if it wasn’t premeditated—if God had let it happen as an accident—then I’ll assign a place where the one who hit them can flee to.
\v 14 \add However,\add* if someone had schemed to trap and kill their neighbour, then \add even if the killer clings to\add* my altar, you must take them for execution.
\p
\v 15 Anyone who strikes their father or mother must certainly be put to death.
\p
\v 16 Anyone who abducts another person—either to sell or to keep \add as a slave\add*—that person must certainly be put to death.
\p
\v 17 Anyone who curses their father or mother must certainly be put to death.
\p
\v 18 If two people fight and one hits their neighbour with a stone or with their fist, and the injured one doesn’t die but is bedridden,
\v 19 if that person \add eventually\add* rises and can walk about outside using a staff, then the person who struck them is free, other than reimbursing the costs of their lost work and their medical expenses.
\p
\v 20 If a master hits their male or female slave with a staff and the slave dies, then the death must certainly be avenged.
\v 21 However, if the slave is able to stand within a couple of days, then the \add owner\add* won’t be punished because the slave is their property.
\p
\v 22 If two people fight together and strike a pregnant woman and cause her to deliver the baby, if there’s no serious injury, then he must certainly be fined whatever the husband of the woman demands and the judges allow.
\v 23 But if there’s serious injury, then you must give a life for a life,
\v 24 an eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth, a hand for a hand, a foot for a foot,
\v 25 a burn for a burn, a wound for a wound, and a bruise for a bruise.
\p
\v 26 If a master hits their male or female slave’s eye and damages it, they must let the slave go free in compensation for the eye.
\v 27 \add Or\add* if the master knocks out a tooth of a male or female slave, they must let the slave go free in compensation for the tooth.
\s1 Rules about property
\p
\v 28 If a bull gores a man or a woman and they die, the bull must certainly be killed by throwing rocks at it. The owner of the animal is \add to be considered\add* innocent, \add but\add* its meat mustn’t be eaten.
\v 29 But if the bull had a habit of goring in the past and its owner was warned but didn’t keep it \add fenced\add* in, and it kills a man or a woman, then the bull must be killed by throwing rocks at it and its owner must also be put to death.
\v 30 If a ransom payment is demanded instead, the owner may buy back their life by paying the full amount demanded.
\v 31 Those rules also apply if the bull gores a \add person’s\add* son or daughter.
\v 32 If the bull gores a male or female slave, its owner must give thirty silver shekels to the \add slave owner\add* and the bull must be killed by throwing rocks at it.
\p
\v 33 If someone uncovers a \add pit for storage\add*, or digs a \add new one\add* and leaves it uncovered, and a cow or a donkey falls into it,
\v 34 the owner of the pit must pay compensation. The pit owner must pay in cash, \add but\add* the dead animal will then belong to them.
\v 35 If one person’s bull attacks \add someone else’s\add* bull and it dies, then they should sell the live animal and divide the money as well as dividing up the dead animal.
\v 36 \add But\add* if it was known that the bull had a habit of goring in the past and its owner hadn’t kept it \add fenced\add* in, then that owner must certainly give a live bull for the dead one, \add but\add* the dead animal will become theirs.
\c 22
\s1 Rules about loss of property or farm animals
\p
\v 1 If someone steals a cow or a sheep and slaughters it or sells it, he must compensate five cattle for the cow or four sheep for the sheep.
\p
\v 2 (If a thief is discovered \add at night\add* while breaking in, and is struck and dies, no one should be charged for murder.
\v 3 But if it’s daylight already, then that person is guilty of murder.)
\p A thief who’s caught must make restitution, but if they’re unable to pay, then the thief must be sold \add to cover it\add*.
\v 4 If a thief is found in the possession of a stolen animal, be it a cow or a donkey or a sheep, then they must pay back another matching animal as well.\f + \fr 22:4 \ft Or possibly, must pay back \+bd two\+bd* additional animals.\f*
\p
\v 5 If a person releases their livestock to graze \add their own\add* field or vineyard, and it \add ends up\add* grazing in someone else’s field, the owner of the animal must compensate from the best of their field and from the best of their vineyard.
\p
\v 6 If a fire breaks out and spreads in the bush, and grain that’s already stacked or is still in the field gets burnt up, the person who started the fire must certainly pay compensation.
\p
\v 7 If anyone gives their neighbour money or goods for safekeeping, and if it’s \add then\add* stolen from the neighbour’s house, if the thief is found, they must pay double in compensation.
\v 8 If the thief can’t be found, then the owner of the house must be brought to the judges for God’s decision as to whether or not they have improperly handled their neighbour’s property.
\p
\v 9 Concerning every other squabble about any cattle or a donkey or sheep, or about clothing or any lost property that’s under dispute, both parties must go to the judges for God’s decision—anyone who God declares to be guilty must pay his neighbour double as compensation.
\p
\v 10 If anyone gives their neighbour a donkey or a cow or a sheep or any animal to look after, and it dies or is injured or disappears without anyone seeing,
\v 11 both of them must declare in front of Yahweh as to whether or not they improperly handled their neighbour’s property. The owner must accept the sworn statement and no compensation is required,
\v 12 \add but\add* if it was truly stolen from the neighbour, they must pay compensation to the owner.
\v 13 If it was truly killed by some other animal, the neighbour must bring the remains as evidence, and no compensation is required.
\p
\v 14 If anyone borrows an animal from their neighbour and it gets injured or dies without the owner being with it, the person who borrowed it must pay compensation,
\v 15 \add but\add* if its owner was \add there\add* with it, no compensation is required—if it was for hire, then that was included in its hiring fee.
\s1 More rules about everyday life
\p
\v 16 If a man entices a young woman who’s not engaged, and sleeps with her, he must certainly pay the bride price \add to her father\add* for her to become his wife.
\v 17 If her father absolutely refuses to give her to him, the man must \add still\add* pay the normal bride price in cash.
\p
\v 18 You must execute any woman who practices sorcery.
\p
\v 19 Anyone who has sex with an animal must be executed.
\p
\v 20 You must execute anyone who offers a sacrifice to any god other than Yahweh.
\p
\v 21 You mustn’t disadvantage or oppress any foreigner staying in your country, because you were foreigners staying in Egypt.
\v 22 You mustn’t mistreat any widow or any fatherless child,
\v 23 \add because\add* if you do and they ask me to help them, I’ll help them
\v 24 and get angry. I’ll cause you all to die in battle, and your wives will become widows and your children will become fatherless.
\p
\v 25 If you loan money to any of my people who are poor, you mustn’t act like an \add impartial\add* creditor to them—you mustn’t charge them interest.
\v 26 If you actually take your neighbour’s cloak as a security, you must return it to them by sunset,
\v 27 because it’s their only covering. What else could they cover themselves with when they try to sleep? If they cry out to me, I’ll act on it, because I’m compassionate.
\p
\v 28 You mustn’t insult God, and don’t curse your leaders.
\p
\v 29 You mustn’t \add fail to give\add* me the best parts of the grain that you harvest, or \add the best\add* \add olive\add* oil or wine that you produce.
\p You must give your firstborn sons to me,
\v 30 plus you must do the same with your cattle and your sheep and goats. The young must stay seven days with their mothers, then you must give them to me on the eighth day.
\p
\v 31 You must be different from others because you’re dedicated to me. \add So\add* you mustn’t eat the meat of animals that are mauled in the countryside—throw it to the dogs \add instead\add*.
\c 23
\s1 Rules about honesty and kindness
\p
\v 1 You mustn’t present a false statement. Don’t conspire with a wicked person to give false evidence.
\v 2 Don’t \add just\add* follow the crowd into doing what’s wrong, and don’t \add just\add* say what others say in court \add if it’s untrue\add*,
\v 3 and don’t \add automatically\add* favour the poor in court either.
\p
\v 4 If you come across your enemy’s cow or donkey going astray, you must certainly take it back to them.
\v 5 If you see that the donkey of someone who hates you has fallen to the ground under its load, you mustn’t just walk way—you must certainly help them with it.
\p
\v 6 You mustn’t deny justice to the poor in their lawsuits.
\v 7 Stay far way from deceptive words. You mustn’t kill innocent or honest people because I won’t \add allow\add* wicked people to be declared innocent.
\v 8 You mustn’t accept bribes, because bribes stop people from being unbiased and they undermine the words of honest people.
\p
\v 9 You mustn’t oppress foreigners since you yourselves know how that feels because you all were foreigners in Egypt.
\s1 The seventh year and the seventh day
\p
\v 10 You should plant your land and gather its produce for six years,
\v 11 \add but\add* in the seventh year you must let the harvest drop and \add just\add* leave it there and \add allow\add* the poor people to eat it. Then the animals in the countryside can eat the reminder. You must \add also\add* do that to your vineyards and your olive orchards.
\p
\v 12 Six days You should work for six days, then on the seventh day you must rest so that your cow and your donkey can rest, and the children of your slaves and the foreigner \add staying in your land\add* can be refreshed.
\p
\v 13 Take careful notice of everything that I’ve told you all. You all mustn’t \add invoke\add* other gods—\add in fact\add* don’t even mention their names.
\s1 Three compulsory celebrations each year
\r (Exo. 34:18-26; Deu. 16:1-17)
\p
\v 14 You must celebrate me three times every year.
\v 15 \add 1/\add* You must observe the \it Flat Bread Celebration\it*. You must eat unleavened bread for seven days as I commanded you, in late March or early April because that’s when you came out from Egypt. You mustn’t come without an offering.
\p
\v 16 \add 2/\add* You must observe the \it \+add Spring\+add* Harvest Celebration\it*, bringing the first portion of your harvests from what you planted.
\p \add 3/\add* You must observe the \it \+add Finished\+add* Harvest Celebration\it* at the end of the year, after you’ve finished harvesting everything you planted.
\v 17 \add So\add* all your males must gather in front of the master Yahweh three times each year.
\p
\v 18 You mustn’t sacrifice the blood of my sacrifices together with bread that’s been risen, and all the fat \add must be burnt during the night\add* so that none remains in the morning.
\p
\v 19 You must bring the best of the firstfruits of your harvests to the house of Yahweh your god.
\p You mustn’t boil a young goat in its mother’s milk.
\s1 Promises and teachings
\p
\v 20 Listen, I’m about to send a messenger in front of you to guard you on the way, and to take you to the place that I have prepared.
\v 21 Stay behind him and listen to what he says. Don’t annoy him because he won’t put up with disobedience because he represents my authority.
\v 22 \add But\add* if you take careful notice of his words and do everything that I say, then I’ll be an enemy to your enemies and I’ll be against those who are against you.
\v 23 My messenger will go ahead of you and take you to \add face\add* the Amorites, the Hittites, the Perizim, the Canaanites, the Hivim, and the Jebusites, and then I’ll annihilate them.
\v 24 You mustn’t serve or bow down to their gods, and you mustn’t do what those people do, because you must completely overthrow them and completely smash their \add sacred\add* \add stone\add* pillars.
\v 25 You all must worship your god Yahweh, and he’ll bless your food and your water. I’ll keep sickness away from you
\v 26 \add and\add* there won’t be any miscarriages or women in your land that can’t conceive. I’ll ensure that you live your full number of days.
\p
\v 27 I’ll go ahead of you and will terrify and confuse all of the people \add groups\add* whose lands you’ll enter, and I’ll cause them to flee from you in battle.
\v 28 I’ll send hornets ahead of you and they’ll drive out the Hivim, the Canaanites, and the Hittites before you get there.
\v 29 I won’t drive them out more than a year ahead of you in case the area becomes desolate and the wild animals proliferate too much in the countryside.
\v 30 I’ll drive them out little by little ahead of you until your numbers increase and you take over the land.
\v 31 Then I’ll help you establish your borders from the Red sea to the Mediterranean, and from the wilderness to the \add Euphrates\add* river, because I’ll help you defeat the existing inhabitants of the land and so you’ll drive them right out.
\v 32 You mustn’t make any agreement with those people or with their gods.
\v 33 They mustn’t live in your land, in case they cause you to sin against me. Their gods would certainly become a trap for you, and then you’d \add end up\add* worshipping them.
\c 24
\s1 The people confirm their agreement
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Come up \add the mountain\add* to me—you, Aaron, Nadav, Avihu and seventy of the Israelite elders, and you must \add stay\add* at a distance to worship.
\v 2 Then Moses alone must approach me, \add but\add* the others mustn’t come near, and \add also\add* the people mustn’t come up with him.”
\p
\v 3 So Moses went and explained to the people everything that Yahweh had said, including all the regulations. Then all of the people answered in unison, “We’ll do everything that Yahweh has told us to do.”
\v 4 So Moses wrote down everything that Yahweh had instructed. Then he got up early in the morning and built an altar at the bottom of the mountain and \add set up\add* twelve \add stone\add* pillars for the twelve tribes of Israel,
\v 5 and he sent some young men to offer burnt offerings and to sacrifice some bulls as peace offerings to Yahweh.
\v 6 Moses took half of the blood \add from the sacrifices\add* and put it into basins. He sprinkled \add the other\add* half of the blood onto the altar.
\v 7 Then he took the scroll with the agreement \add written on it\add* and read it out loud to the people, and they responded, “We’ll obey everything that Yahweh has instructed.”
\p
\v 8 Then Moses took the blood \add in the bowls\add* and sprinkled it onto the people, telling them, “Look. \add This is\add* the blood \add confirming\add* the agreement that Yahweh has made with you according to what you just heard.”
\p
\v 9 Then Moses, Aaron, Nadav, Avihu, and seventy Israelite elders climbed up \add the mountain\add*
\v 10 and they saw the god of Israel. Under his feet was something like a tile made of sapphire that looked so pure that it was as clear as the sky.
\v 11 Those Israelite leaders saw God and yet they weren’t harmed by him.. Then they ate and drank \add together\add*.
\s1 Moses’s long stay on Mt. Sinai
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Come up to me on the mountain and \add wait\add* there, and I will give you some stone slabs with the rules and regulations that I have written \add on them so you can\add* to teach them to the people.”
\v 13 So Moses and his assistant Joshua set off, and Moses climbed up God’s mountain.
\v 14 He had \add already\add* told the elders, “Wait for us \add down here\add* in this place until we return \add here\add* to you all. And see, Aaron and Hur are with you—any leader judging matters requiring more wisdom can approach them \add for help\add*.”
\p
\v 15 Then Moses went to \add the base of\add* the mountain and a cloud covered \add over\add* it.
\v 16 Yahweh’s radiance settled on Mt. Sinai, and the cloud covered it for six days, then on the seventh day he called to Moses from within the cloud.
\v 17 The appearance of Yahweh’s radiance was like a roaring fire on the top of the mountain from what the Israelites below could see.
\v 18 Then Moses entered \add into\add* the cloud and continued up the mountain, and he remained up there for forty full days.
\c 25
\s1 Contributions for God’s tent
\r (Exo. 35:4-9)
\p
\v 1 Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Tell the Israelites that they should raise funds for me. You should collect a contribution from every family that’s willing to donate.
\v 3 You should accept contributions of gold, silver, or bronze,
\v 4 as well as yarn \add dyed\add* blue, purple, or crimson, plus finely-spun linen and \add spun\add* goat hair,
\v 5 and red-dyed ram skins, fine leather, and acacia timber.
\v 6 \add Also\add* lamp oil, spices for \add making\add* anointing oil and fragrant incense,
\v 7 onyx and \add other\add* gemstones for setting into the apron and the breastplate.
\v 8 Then they should make me a residence \add so\add* I can live \add in it\add* among them,
\v 9 according to the patterns that I’ll show you for the residence and for all of its utensils. You all must make all of it exactly to plan.
\s1 The box to hold the stone slabs
\r (Exo. 37:1-9)
\p
\v 10 They must make a wooden chest from acacia wood. It should be 125cm long, with the width and the height being 75cm.
\v 11 It must be overlaid with pure gold \add sheeting\add* on the outside and the inside, and have a gold moulding \add fitted\add* all around it.
\v 12 Four gold rings must be cast and attached to the four feet—two rings on each side.
\v 13 Make two \add carrying\add* poles from acacia wood and overlay them with gold \add sheeting\add*.
\v 14 Those poles will be inserted through the rings on the sides of the chest so it can be \add picked up and\add* carried on them.
\v 15 The poles must be kept in the rings on the chest—they mustn’t be removed.
\v 16 The two stone slabs that I’ll give you with our agreement inscribed on them must be placed inside the chest.
\p
\v 17 You must make a lid the same length and width as the chest. It must be made of pure gold \add and will be known as\add* ‘the atonement platform’.
\v 18 Then make a pair of winged-creatures from gold that’s hammered from the two ends of the atonement platform.
\v 19 \add Yes,\add* make one winged-creature from each end of the gold lid material.
\v 20 Their up-lifted wings should spread out covering the atonement platform and they should be facing each other across the top of the platform.
\v 21 When I give you the \add stone\add* slabs, put them inside the box and then fasten the atonement platform on top to form the lid.
\v 22 Then I’ll meet with you there, and from the atonement platform between the two winged-creatures and above the \add stone\add* slabs, I’ll be able to tell you my commands to pass on to the Israelites.
\s1 The bread offering table
\r (Exo. 37:10-16)
\p
\v 23 You must make a table from acacia wood: one metre long, 500cm wide, and 750cm high.
\v 24 Overlay it with pure gold and have a gold moulding fitted all around it.
\v 25 Make an 8cm wide rim around it and fit a gold moulding onto the rim.
\v 26 Make four gold rings and attach them near the legs on the four corners \add of the table\add*
\v 27 near the rim. The rings are so poles can be inserted in order to carry the table.
\v 28 Make \add two\add* poles out of acacia wood and overlaid them with gold for carrying the table.
\v 29 \add In addition,\add* make the necessary plates and pans, and the pitchers and bowls to be used for pouring out the wine offerings—all from pure gold.
\v 30 You must keep \add sacred\add* bread on the table continually in front of me.
\s1 The lampstand
\r (Exo. 37:17-24)
\p
\v 31 You must make a lampstand from pure gold. The base and shaft are to be hammered out \add of one piece of gold\add* as well as its cups and \add decorative\add* buds and flowers.
\v 32 There must be six branches extending from the shaft—three on each side.
\v 33 Three cups shaped like almond flowers must be on each branch along with a bud and petals.
\v 34 The \add centre\add* shaft must have four cups like almond flowers along with a bud and petals.
\v 35 For each of the three pairs of branches \add (one branch out each side in each pair)\add*, make a bud \add on the centre shaft\add* so each pair of branches appear to be growing out of it.
\v 36 These buds and their branches must all be hammered out from \add that\add* single piece of pure gold.
\v 37 Make seven lamps and set them up on it to give light out from the front of it,
\v 38 and make all the utensils and dishes for the lamps from pure gold.
\v 39 This can all be made from 35 kilograms of pure gold
\v 40 and be sure to make it all like the pattern that you’re being shown on the mountain.
\c 26
\s1 Plans for the sacred residence
\r (Exo. 36:8-38)
\p
\v 1 You must make my residence with ten fabric panels formed by a skilled craftsman. They must be made from twisted finely-spun linen, with winged-creatures \add embroidered on them using\add* blue, purple, and scarlet thread.
\v 2 The hanging panels must all be the same size: 14.5m long and two metres wide.
\v 3 Five of the panels will be coupled together \add as one set\add* and the other five as another.
\v 4 For each set, make loops of blue cloth and fasten them along the outer edge of each of the end panels—
\v 5 place fifty loops on the end of each set so that the loops are opposite each other.
\v 6 You’ll also need to make fifty gold clips to join the two sets together to make a single walled structure.
\p
\v 7 You must make eleven panels with goats’ hair to form a tent over the residence.
\v 8 Each of the eleven panels must be the same size: 15m long and two metres wide.
\v 9 You must join five panels into a set, and six panels into the other set so that the sixth panel can be doubled over at the front of the tent.
\v 10 You must make 50 loops to go onto the end of one set, and another 50 for the edge of the other set.
\v 11 You’ll also need to make fifty bronze clips to join the two sets together to make the tent cover.
\v 12 Let the extra half panel hang over the back of the residence.
\v 13 The half metre of extra length on each side \add that extends beyond the linen sets\add* will hang \add evenly\add* over the sides.
\p
\v 14 You must make a covering of rams hides dyed red to go over the tent, then another covering sea-cow hides to go over that.
\s2 The wooden frames
\p
\v 15 You must make frames for my residence from acacia wood. They’ll stand upright
\v 16 \add and\add* each one must be five metres high and 0.75m wide.
\v 17 Each frame must have two protrusions so it can be connected to the frame next to it.
\v 18 Assemble twenty frames for the south side of the residence
\v 19 along with forty silver bases to go underneath them—two bases supporting each frame, and each frame having two protrusions at the bottom to go into the bases.
\v 20 For the north side, assemble twenty frames
\v 21 along with forty silver bases—two under each frame.
\v 22 For the rear of the residence facing west, assemble six frames
\v 23 plus two extra frames for \add bracing\add* the two back corners—
\v 24 they must be joined \add on\add* at the bottom and then both of them have their tops attached to a ring.
\v 25 Then assemble eight frames with their sixteen silver bases—two bases supporting each frame.
\p
\v 26 Make crossbars from acacia wood—five of them to go across the frames on the northern side,
\v 27 five for the south side, and five for the frames at the rear of the residence on the western side.
\v 28 The middle crossbar in the centre of the frames will go right through from one end to the other.
\v 29 Overlay the frames and the crossbars with gold, and make the rings for holding the crossbars with gold.
\v 30 Then you must assemble my residence exactly as per the plan that you were shown on the mountain.
\s2 The curtain
\p
\v 31 You must have a skillful workman make a curtain from twisted finely-spun linen, with winged-creatures \add embroidered on them using\add* blue, purple, and scarlet thread.
\v 32 Hang it from gold clips on four pillars made from acacia wood overlaid with gold on four silver bases.
\v 33 Hang the curtain under the clips, then bring the box that contains the agreement inside the curtain. That curtain will separate for you between the sacred section and the \em very\em* sacred interior.
\v 34 Install the atonement platform as the lid on the box there in the very sacred interior.
\v 35 Set the table up outside the curtain on the north side, with the lampstand on the south side opposite the table.
\p
\v 36 Then make a curtain out of twisted finely-spun linen for the entrance of the tent. It should be embroidered with blue, purple and scarlet yarn.
\v 37 Make five pillars for the curtain from acacia wood overlaid with gold. The hooks on them must be made of gold, and their five bases must be cast with bronze.
\c 27
\s1 The altar for burnt offerings
\r (Exo. 38:1-7)
\p
\v 1 Make an altar from acacia wood that’s 2.5m square and 1.5m high
\v 2 and as part of that same piece of wood, include \add a carved projection like\add* a horn on each of the top corners of the altar. Overlay the altar with bronze.
\v 3 Make pots for the ashes out of bronze, along with the necessary shovels, sprinkling bowls, meat forks, and fire-trays.
\v 4 Then make a bronze grating for it with four rings of bronze on the four corners.
\v 5 Fit it down onto a ledge inside the altar so that it sits inside the altar about halfway up.
\v 6 \add Also,\add* make poles from acacia wood for the altar and overlay them with bronze.
\v 7 They’ll be able to be put into rings on two sides of the altar for carrying it.
\v 8 The altar should be hollow in the middle and made from \add wooden\add* planks just like you were shown on the mountain.
\s1 The courtyard around the sacred tent
\r (Exo. 38:9-20)
\p
\v 9 Make curtains of twisted finely-spun linen \add to form\add* a courtyard \add around\add* the residence. The south side will require fifty metres of curtain
\v 10 with twenty \add bronze\add* pillars and their twenty bases. The hooks on the pillars and the clips must be made of silver.
\v 11 Similarly on the north side, \add another\add* fifty metres of curtain with twenty pillars and their bases and their hooks and clips.
\v 12 The west side will require twenty-five metres of curtain with ten pillars and bases.
\v 13 The east side of the courtyard must also be twenty-five metres wide.
\v 14 On one side of the gate, the curtain will be 7.5m long with three pillars and bases,
\v 15 and the same on the other side.
\v 16 The entrance curtain must be made of twisted finely-spun linen. It’ll be ten metres wide and hang on four pillars with their bases, and must be embroidered with blue, purple, and scarlet yarn.
\v 17 All the pillars for the courtyard must have silver decorations around them, as well as their silver hooks and bronze bases.
\v 18 \add So\add* the courtyard will be fifty metres by twenty-five metres and the curtain enclosure will be 2.5m high. All the curtains will be made of finely-spun linen, and the bases under all the pillars will be bronze.
\v 19 All the equipment for the operation of the residence and all the tent pegs and pegs for the courtyard must be made of bronze.
\s1 The oil for the lamps
\r (Lev. 24:1-4)
\p
\v 20 Order the Israelites to bring you pure, pressed olive oil for the lamps so they can be kept burning continually.
\v 21 In the outer portion of the \add sacred\add* tent (outside the curtain where the \add sacred\add* box is kept), Aaron and his sons must attend to the lampstand in front of Yahweh from evening to morning. This regulation will apply continually to all future generations of Israelites.
\c 28
\s1 The priests’ uniform
\r (Exo. 39:1-7)
\p
\v 1 \add Now,\add* have your brother Aaron and his sons brought to you from among the Israelites. Aaron, Nadav, Avihu, Eleazar, and Itamar will become my priests.
\v 2 Make sacred clothes for Aaron—they must be \add uniquely\add* beautiful
\v 3 so speak to all the skilled craftsmen who’ve been given the spirit of wisdom, and they can make Aaron’s clothes to dedicate him as my priest.
\v 4 These are the clothes that they’ll need to make: a small pouch to hang around the neck and an apron, a robe and a tunic of woven work, and a turban and a waistband. They must make \add those\add* sacred clothes for your brother Aaron and for his sons to serve as my priests.
\v 5 The skilled craftsmen must be given gold \add wire\add* and blue, purple, and scarlet yarn, and finely-spun linen.
\p
\v 6 They must make the sacred apron from twisted finely-spun linen with gold \add wire\add* and blue, purple, scarlet yarn embroidered onto it by a skilled craftsman.
\v 7 It should have two shoulder pieces attached to \add the top\add* two corners so it can be fastened.
\v 8 Then the waistband for the apron should be made with similar workmanship from twisted finely-spun linen with gold \add wire\add* and blue, purple, and scarlet yarn skillfully embroidered into it.
\v 9 Then get two onyx stones and engrave the names of Israel’s \add twelve\add* sons onto them—
\v 10 six names on one stone and six on the other, in their birth order.
\v 11 A stone craftsman with experience at making signet rings should do the engravings, and then mount the two stones in decorative gold settings.
\v 12 Then attach the two stones to the shoulder pieces of the apron as a reminder about Israel’s sons. That way Aaron will carry their names on his two shoulders as a reminder \add every time\add* he goes to face Yahweh.
\v 13 Make the decorative settings for the \add two\add* stones from gold,
\v 14 and then make two twisted chains of pure gold like a cord that you can attach to the decorative settings.
\s1 The chestpiece
\r (Exo. 39:8-21)
\p
\v 15 Make a \bd \+add sacred\+add* chest pouch\bd* for important decision making. A skilled craftsman should make it from twisted finely-spun linen like the apron, embroidered with gold \add wire\add* and blue, purple, and scarlet yarn.
\v 16 It needs to be 23cm square when folded over double,
\v 17 and it will have four rows of \add three\add* gemstones on it: a \add red\add* ruby, a \add yellow\add* topaz, a \add green\add* emerald,
\v 18 then a turquoise, a \add blue\add* sapphire, a diamond,
\v 19 then a \add red\add* jacinth, a \add white\add* agate, a \add purple\add* amethyst,
\v 20 and in the last row, a \add yellow\add* beryl, an onyx, and a \add green\add* jasper. All those stones must be mounted in decorative gold settings,
\v 21 and \add so\add* they’ll represent the twelve tribes of Israel. The names of the twelve sons must be engraved on the stones similar to how signet rings \add are made\add*.
\v 22 Make two twisted chains of pure gold like a cord that you can attach to the pouch,
\v 23 then make two gold rings and attach them to the two \add top\add* corners of the pouch.
\v 24 Attach the two gold chains to the rings on the corners
\v 25 with the other ends fastened to the two decorative settings \add around the stones\add* and then attached to the front side of the shoulder straps.
\v 26 Make two more gold rings and attach them on the inside to the two \add bottom\add* corners of the pouch,
\v 27 and another two gold rings to be attached to the bottom of the two shoulder pieces near where the shoulder straps join the apron just above the crafted waistband.
\v 28 Then tie the rings on the sacred pouch to the rings on the apron with a blue cord so that the pouch sits above the waistband and can’t come loose from the apron.
\p
\v 29 Then \add whenever\add* Aaron enters the sacred inner room \add to stand\add* in front of me, he’ll be carrying the names of Israel’s sons over his heart on the sacred pouch that’ll be used for making decisions.
\v 30 Then place the ‘Urim’ and the ‘Tumim’\f + \fr 28:30 \ft These two objects appear abruptly in the text—they haven’t been described previously—in fact, they’re not described anywhere, so we don’t really know much about them except that it seems they were used somehow to indicate Yahweh’s decision on important matters. Although we presume that Moses must have already been familiar with them, sadly we’re unable to translate these two Hebrew words, because we’re not.\f* in the sacred pouch so they’ll be over Aaron’s heart when he goes in before Yahweh, thus Aaron will continually bear the decisions of the Israelites over his heart before Yahweh.
\s1 The other parts of the uniform
\r (Exo. 39:22-31)
\p
\v 31 Make the \bd robe\bd* \add to be worn under\add* the sacred apron entirely from blue material
\v 32 with an opening for his head in the middle of it. A border will be needed around the opening so it can’t tear—this should be woven like openings in regular clothes.
\v 33 Then on the bottom hem, make \add decorations that look like\add* pomegranates—they must be made from blue, purple, and scarlet yarn. Gold bells should hang in between the pomegranates
\v 34 so as you go around the hem of the robe there’ll be a gold bell, then a pomegranate, then a gold bell, then a pomegranate, etc.
\v 35 Aaron must wear this robe when he’s serving, so when he goes into my presence in the sacred inner room, I’ll hear the bells when he enters and when he leaves, so he won’t die.
\p
\v 36 Make a plate from pure gold and engrave ‘Dedicated to Yahweh’ on it like you would on a signet ring.
\v 37 Attach it to the front of the turban using a blue cord
\v 38 so it’ll be on Aaron’s forehead. \add In this way,\add* it’ll bear the \add deficiencies of human\add* sinfulness when Israelites \add attempt\add* to make sacred objects and \add attempt\add* to make sacred offerings. Thus it’ll be on his forehead continually so that I will \add see it and\add* accept those things.
\p
\v 39 The \bd tunic\bd* must be woven from finely-spun linen, and similarly for the \bd turban\bd*. An embroiderer must make the \bd waistband\bd*.
\p
\v 40 You must \add also\add* make tunics, waistbands and headbands for Aaron’s sons so they’ll \add look\add* splendid and beautiful.
\v 41 Put these clothes on your brother Aaron and his sons, then anoint them \add with oil\add* and ordain and consecrate them as priests to me.
\v 42 Make \bd underwear\bd* out of linen to cover their bare flesh from the waist to the thighs—
\v 43 they must wear it whenever they enter the \add sacred\add* tent and whenever they approach the altar to offer sacrifices so they won’t be disobeying and die. Aaron and his descendants must obey that rule forever.
\c 29
\s1 Consecrating the priests
\r (Lev. 8:1-36)
\p
\v 1 Now this is how you should consecrate them to be priests to me: Take a young bull from the herd and two rams without defects.
\v 2 \add Also,\add* using finely-ground wheat flour, \add bake\add* flat bread, flat cakes with \add olive\add* oil in them, and flat wafers sprinkled with \add olive\add* oil.
\v 3 Put the baking into a basket, and bring the basket \add to me\add* along with the bull and two rams.
\p
\v 4 Then take Aaron and his sons near to the entrance of the \add sacred\add* tent and wash them with water.
\v 5 Take the clothes and dress Aaron in the tunic and the robe that goes with the apron, then the sacred apron and the sacred pouch, and tie the waistband around the apron.
\v 6 Put the turban on his head and attach the sacred \add gold\add* ornament to it,
\v 7 then take the anointing oil and pour it over his head to dedicate him.
\v 8 \add Next,\add* bring his sons and put tunics on them
\v 9 and wrap waistbands around them and tie on their headbands. They \add and their descendants\add* will be priests forever, and you must ordain \add both\add* Aaron and his sons.
\p
\v 10 Then bring the bull to the front of the \add sacred\add* tent and get Aaron and his sons to put their hands on its head,
\v 11 then slaughter\f + \fr 29:11 \ft The throat will be cut with a sharp knife, and the blood will be caught in a bowl.\f* the bull before me at the entrance of the \add sacred\add* tent.
\v 12 Take some of that blood and smear it on the top projections of the altar with your finger, then pour the rest of it \add on the ground\add* at the base of the altar.
\v 13 Take all the fat covering the innards including the lobe above the liver, as well as both kidneys and the fat on them, and burn it all on the altar,
\v 14 but the bull’s meat and hide and intestines must be burnt outside the camp. Sacrificing \add the bull’s fat like that\add* is an act of purification.
\p
\v 15 Then take one of the rams and have Aaron and his sons place their hands on its head,
\v 16 then slaughter it. \add Catch\add* the blood and sprinkle it all over \add the sides of\add* the altar.
\v 17 Then cut the ram into pieces and wash its innards and its legs, and put those bits \add together\add* with the head and the \add other\add* pieces.
\v 18 Then burn it all on the altar. That will be a burnt offering to me—a pleasing aroma \add from\add* the offering by fire.
\p
\v 19 Then take the second ram, and have Aaron and his sons place their hands on its head,
\v 20 then slaughter it. \add Catch\add* the blood and smear some of it on the tip of Aaron and his sons’ right ears and on their right thumbs and big toes. Then sprinkle the rest all over \add the sides of\add* the altar.
\v 21 Take some of the blood that is on the altar and some of the anointing oil, and sprinkle that on Aaron and his clothes, and on his sons and their clothes. That will consecrate Aaron and his sons and their \add priestly\add* clothes.
\p
\v 22 Take the fat of \add that second\add* ram, and its fat tail, the fat covering its innards and the lobe above the liver, the two kidneys and that fat that’s on them, and the right thigh, \add because\add* this ram is for consecration.
\v 23 \add Also\add* take one \add round\add* bread loaf, one cake of bread made with oil in it, and one wafer from that basket of flat breads that’s \add offered to\add* me.
\v 24 Put all those into the hands of Aaron and his sons, and \add they\add* must wave them before me as a wave offering.
\v 25 Then take them from their hands and burn them completely on the altar on top of the burnt offering. That also will be a fire offering to me, and its aroma will please me.
\p
\v 26 Take the breast meat of the ram (\add the second one\add* for the consecration of Aaron) and wave it as a wave offering before me. Then after that it will be your share \add to cook and eat\add*.
\p
\v 27 So you should consecrate the breast of the wave offering and the thigh of the contribution from the ram of consecration for Aaron and his sons.
\v 28 Those will belong to Aaron and his sons as their regular portion from the Israelites—it will be a contribution from the Israelites from their peace offerings to me.
\p
\v 29 The sacred clothes that belong to Aaron will be used for his male descendants to wear \add in turn\add* when they’re anointed and consecrated.
\v 30 The priest who succeeds him from among his sons, who comes into the \add sacred\add* tent to serve in the sacred inner room must wear them for seven days.
\p
\v 31 Take the ram of consecration and boil its meat in a sacred place,
\v 32 then Aaron and his sons should eat the meat of the ram as well as the breads that are in the basket at the entrance to the tent.
\v 33 They must eat those things used to make atonement for them and to consecrate them. No one else can eat them, because they’ve been made sacred.
\v 34 If any meat of the consecration or any of the bread remains until the morning, then you must burn the remainder—it can’t be eaten because it’s been made sacred.
\p
\v 35 So that’s what you must do for Aaron and his sons, just like I’ve commanded you. You’ll consecrate them for seven days,
\v 36 and each day you must sacrifice a bull to purify the altar and compensate for the altar’s imperfections. You must also anoint it \add each day with olive oil\add* to make it sacred.
\v 37 After making atonement for the altar and making it sacred for seven days, it will then be so sacred that anything that touches it will also become sacred.
\s1 The daily sacrifices
\r (Num. 28:1-8)
\p
\v 38 \add After all that,\add* you must continually offer two, year-old lambs every day—
\v 39 one in the morning and the other around twilight.
\v 40 With the morning lamb, \add also\add* offer two kilograms of finely-ground wheat flour mixed with a litre of pressed \add olive\add* oil and a litre of wine.
\v 41 With the twilight lamb, offer the same grain offering as in the morning, and the same drink offering. That will be a fire offering to me and the aroma will please me.
\v 42 This must be a continual offering to me at the entrance of the \add sacred\add* tent \add even\add* in following generations, and I’ll meet with you all there to speak to you there
\v 43 and I’ll meet with the Israelites there, and my splendour will sanctify it.
\v 44 I’ll consecrate the sacred tent and the altar, and I’ll sanctify Aaron and his sons to be my priests.
\v 45 Then I’ll live \add there\add* among the Israelites and I will be their god,
\v 46 and they will know that I’m their god Yahweh, who brought them out of Egypt so I could live among them. I am Yahweh their god.
\c 30
\s1 The incense altar
\r (Exo. 37:25-28)
\p
\v 1 You must make an altar out of acacia word for burning incense on.
\v 2 It should be square—half a metre on each side—and a metre high. Make projections that look like horns on top out of that same block of wood.
\v 3 It must be overlaid with pure gold—its top and all around its sides and its projections—plus you should make a gold moulding all around it.
\v 4 Make two gold rings and attach them to the altar below the moulding—one on each side to hold the poles for carrying it with.
\v 5 Make the two poles from acacia wood and overlay them with gold.
\v 6 Put this incense altar outside the curtain that hangs in front of the \add sacred\add* chest—the box containing the \add stone\add* slabs and with a platform on it where I will meet with you.
\p
\v 7 Aaron must burn fragrant incense on this altar every morning when he looks after the lamps
\v 8 and \add again\add* every evening when he lights the lamps, \add so\add* incense must be continually burning before me throughout your generations.
\v 9 Don’t offer up any incense or burnt offering or grain offering on it that’s different from these instructions, and don’t pour a drink offering on it.
\v 10 Once a year, Aaron must take the purifying blood from the annual atonement sacrifice and put it on the projections of the altar to compensate for the altar’s imperfection. Each high priest will cover the altar’s flaws this way throughout all future generations. The altar will be very sacred—dedicated to me.
\s1 The tax for making the tent
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\v 12 Whenever you take a census of the Israelite men, each man must pay a ransom to Yahweh for his life when they’re counted, and then the counting won’t cause deadly diseases among them.
\v 13 As each man crosses over to stand with those who’ve been counted, he must pay give a half-shekel coin as an offering to me. (That’s half of the official shekel weight of 12 grams.)
\v 14 Every man who’s twenty or older must pay this contribution to me when he’s counted—
\v 15 the wealthy aren’t expected to pay more, and the poor must not pass less than this amount when they pay this ransom to me to make atonement for your lives.
\v 16 Then you must take that money from the Israelites and use it for work on the \add sacred\add* tent—it will be a reminder to me that you all have made atonement for your lives.
\s1 The washbasin
\p
\v 17 The Yahweh told Moses:
\v 18 Make a bronze washbasin with a bronze base. Place it between the \add sacred\add* tent and the altar, and fill it with water
\v 19 and Aaron and his sons must wash their hands and feet in it.
\v 20 Before they go into the \add sacred\add* tent, they must wash with water so that won’t die, and \add also\add* when they’re going to the altar to burn offerings to me—
\v 21 they must wash their hands and feet so they won’t die. That will be a rule for them and for all their descendants throughout the generations.
\s1 The anointing oil
\p
\v 22 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\v 23 Then you yourself must collect these spices: six kilograms of \add fragrant sap\add* named myrrh, then three kilograms of fragrant cinnamon, three kilograms of fragrant cane,
\v 24 and six kilograms of fragrant casia bark. (Use the official weight for everything.) Also, four litres of olive oil.
\v 25 Get a professional perfumer to make this into a sacred, fragrant, anointing oil.
\v 26 Use it to anoint the \add sacred\add* tent and the box containing the \add stone\add* slabs,
\v 27 as well as the table and all its utensils, the lampstand and its utensils, the incense altar,
\v 28 the altar for burnt offerings and all its equipment, and the basin and its base.
\v 29 That will make them sacred and dedicated to me, and anything that touches them will also become sacred.
\p
\v 30 Then anoint Aaron and his sons, and dedicate them to become my priests.
\v 31 Approach the Israelites and tell them, “This \add recipe\add* will be my sacred anointing oil for all generations.
\v 32 It must not be poured on anyone’s body, and you all must not use any similar recipe \add for yourselves\add*—it’s sacred and must be treated as such.
\v 33 Anyone who mixes together anything like that, or who puts any of it on a stranger, must be expelled from the community.
\s1 The incense
\p
\v 34 Then Yahweh told Moses: Collect equal amounts of stacte, onycha, and galbanum spices, and pure frankincense,
\v 35 and get a professional ointment mixer to add salt and make it into pure incense that will be sacred.
\v 36 Pulverise some of it into a fine powder and put it in front of the box containing the \add stone\add* slabs inside the sacred tent. This incense must be regarded as being very sacred.
\v 37 No one else should make any incense for yourselves with this same recipe—it must be regarded as sacred to me.
\v 38 Anyone who makes anything similar for its fragrance must be expelled from the community.
\c 31
\s1 Betsalel and Oholiav
\r (Exo. 35:30–36:1)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\v 2 Listen, I’ve chosen a man named Betsalel (son of Uri, son of Hur, from Judah’s descendants)
\v 3 and I’ve filled him with my spirit with wisdom, ability, knowledge, and general craftsmanship.
\v 4 \add He’s capable of\add* designing and creating gold, silver, and bronze works,
\v 5 as well as cutting and setting gemstones, wood-carving, and other crafts.
\v 6 And what’s more, I’ve appointed Oholiav (son of Ahisamak, from Dan’s descendants) to help him, and I’ve \add also\add* given \add special\add* ability to other skilled men so that they can make everything that I’ve instructed you:
\li1
\v 7 the \add sacred\add* tent,
\li1 the box containing the \add stone\add* slabs with the atonement platform on top of it,
\li1 all the equipment for inside the \add sacred\add* tent,
\li1
\v 8 the table and its utensils,
\li1 the gold lampstand and all its utensils,
\li1 the altar for \add burning\add* incense,
\li1
\v 9 the altar for the burnt offerings and all its utensils,
\li1 the washbasin with its base,
\li1
\v 10 the woven clothes: the sacred clothes for Aaron the priest and his sons,
\li1
\v 11 the anointing oil, and
\li1 the fragrant incense for the \add sacred\add* tent.
\m Those craftsmen must make everything \add exactly\add* according to my instructions.
\s1 The rest day
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\v 13 Tell the Israelites that you all must certainly observe the \add weekly\add* rest day because the rest day is a sign between me and you all throughout your generations, so that you will know that I am Yahweh, who wants to distinguish you from other nations.
\v 14 So you all must observe the rest day because it’s sacred for you. Anyone who treats it as an ordinary day must be put to death. Anyone who works on it must be expelled from the community.
\v 15 There’s six days for working, and then the seventh is a rest day—dedicated to me. Anyone who works on it must certainly be put to death.
\v 16 The Israelites must observe the rest day for all future generations. There’s no ending to this agreement
\v 17 between me and the Israelites. It’s a never-ending sign that I made the heavens and the earth in six days, and then rested on the seventh day and was refreshed.
\p
\v 18 When Yahweh finished speaking to Moses, he gave Moses the two stone slabs with the transcript that he had engraved on them with his own finger.
\c 32
\s1 The gold bull idol
\r (Deu. 9:6-29)
\p
\v 1 \add Meanwhile,\add* the people had noticed that Moses had been up on the mountain for a long time, \add so\add* they gathered themselves around Aaron and told him, “Come on. Make some gods for us that can go ahead of us, because we don’t know what’s happened to that Moses—the man\f + \fr 32:1 \ft The way that the people speak of Moses here suggests that maybe they never really ever regarded him as being one of them—remember, he did grow up in an Egyptian palace.\f* who brought us out of Egypt.”
\p
\v 2 Aaron replied, “Go and get the gold earrings off your wives and your sons and daughters, and bring them to me.”
\v 3 So the people took off all their gold earrings and brought them to Aaron,
\v 4 and he took them and smelted and crafted the gold into the form of a young bull. Then the people said, “These are your gods,\f + \fr 32:4 \ft After crafting the gold into the form of a young bull (which wasn’t necessarily solid but may have had an internal wooden frame or a clay centre), it’s not clear here why the text refers to plural ‘gods’.\f* Israel, who brought you out of Egypt.”
\p
\v 5 When Aaron saw that, he built an altar in front of the bull and announced, “Tomorrow will be a festival to \add honour\add* Yahweh.”
\v 6 So they got up early the next day and offered burnt offerings and brought peace offerings. Then the people sat down to eat and drink, then stood up to make merry.
\p
\v 7 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Go on down because your people that you brought out of Egypt have gone bad—
\v 8 they’ve quickly deviated from what I instructed them. They’ve made a metal bull for themselves and have bowed down to it and sacrificed to it and they’ve said, ‘Israel, these are your gods that brought you out of Egypt.’ ”
\v 9 Then Yahweh said to Moses, “I’ve seen this people, and look, they’re so stubborn.
\v 10 So leave me alone now and let my anger rage against them. I will destroy them and make you into a great nation.”
\v 11 But Moses pleaded with his god Yahweh and asked, “Yahweh, why are you angry at your people that you have brought out of Egypt with your incredible power and actions?
\v 12 If you did that, the Egyptians would say, ‘\add Ha,\add* he brought them out with evil plans to kill them in the mountains and to destroy them from off the earth.’ Turn from your anger and repent from \add any idea of doing\add* evil to your people.
\v 13 Remember Abraham and Isaac and Israel—your servants that you swore to them yourself and told them, ‘I will cause your descendants to increase like the stars in the sky, and I will give your descendants all this land which I told you about, and they will inherit it forever.’ ”
\v 14 So Yahweh repented from the evil that he’d said he’d do to his people.
\p
\v 15 Then Moses turned and went down the mountain carrying the two tablets with the transcript. The tablets were engraved on both sides—front and back.
\v 16 They were God’s work—it was his writing and he had inscribed it on the tablets.
\v 17 \add As they descended,\add* Joshua heard the sounds of people shouting and said to Moses, “It sounds like a war going on down there in the camp!”
\v 18 But Moses responded, “It’s not a victory march, and it’s not moans of defeat—it’s singing that I can hear!”
\v 19 Then as they approached the camp, they saw the bull and the dancing, and Moses got very angry and threw the tablets down at the bottom of the mountain and they shattered.
\v 20 He took the bull they had made and burnt it in the fire, then he ground it \add down\add* until it was powder and sprinkled it on top of \add pots of\add* water and made the Israelites drink it.
\p
\v 21 Then Moses demanded from Aaron, “What did these people do to you to make you bring such a terrible sin on them?”
\v 22 Aaron responded, “Don’t be angry with me, my master. You know the people—they’re intent on evil.
\v 23 They insisted, saying, ‘Make a god for us to lead us. As for that Moses—the man that brought us out of Egypt—we don’t know what’s happened to him.’
\v 24 So I told them, ‘Anyone who has gold \add jewellery\add*, take it off yourself,’ and they gave it to me and I threw it into the fire, and this bull came out.”
\p
\v 25 Moses saw that the people were out of control because Aaron had let them go wild, \add and now they were subject\add* to mockery from any group that stood up against them.
\v 26 Then Moses stood at the camp entrance and asked, “Anyone who’s for Yahweh, come to me.” And all the \add men from the\add* clan of Levi gathered around him.
\v 27 Then he told them, “This is what Yahweh, the god of Israel, says: ‘Each man must strap his sword on his side. Pass through and return from gate to gate in the camp and kill the unfaithful men—even if it’s your brother, or friend, or neighbour.’ ”
\v 28 The Levite men did what Moses ordered and they killed about three thousand Israelite men that day.
\v 29 Then Moses told them, “Consecrate yourselves to Yahweh today, because any man who \add stood\add* against his son or his brother today \add has\add* placed a blessing on himself.”
\p
\v 30 The next day, Moses told the people, “You all have committed a terrible sin, but now I will go up to Yahweh—perhaps I can atone for your sin.”
\v 31 So Moses returned to Yahweh and said, “Please, these people have committed a terrible sin and made gods for themselves out of gold.
\v 32 But now, maybe you’ll forgive their sin, but if not, please blot me out of your book that you’ve written.”
\v 33 But Yahweh said to Moses, “It’s anyone who sinned against me that I’ll blot out of my book.
\v 34 So go now \add and\add* lead the people to where I’ve spoken to you. See, my angel will go ahead of you, but on the day that I settle accounts, then I’ll punish them for their sin.”
\p
\v 35 Then Yahweh sent deadly diseases onto the people because they had got Aaron to make the \add gold\add* bull.
\c 33
\s1 God tells them to leave Mt. Sinai
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Go \add and\add* pack up from here, you and the people that you brought out of Egypt. Go to the land which I promised to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob, when I told them that I’d give it to their descendants.
\v 2 I’ll send an angel ahead of you, and I’ll drive out the Canaanites, the Amorites, the Hittites, the Perizim, the Hivim, and the Jebusites.
\v 3 \add It’s\add* a very fertile land, but I won’t stay among you in case I \add decide to\add* destroy you on the way because you’re stubborn people.”
\p
\v 4 \add When\add* the people heard that angry comment, they mourned and none of them put on anything fancy.\f + \fr 33:4 \ft But they didn’t mourn enough to wear sackcloth and throw ashes on themselves.\f*
\v 5 Yahweh \add had\add*\f + \fr 33:5 \ft Assumed.\f* told Moses, “Tell the Israelites, ‘You’re all stubborn people. If I went with you for \add even\add* one moment, then I’d destroy you. So now, take off your jewellery and anything fancy that you’re wearing, and then I’ll know what I’ll do to you.’ ”
\v 6 So from Mt. Sinai \add onwards\add*, the Israelites stripped themselves of their jewellery and fancy things.
\s1 The tent for meeting God
\p
\v 7 \add From that day onwards,\add* Moses would take a tent and would put it up at a distance outside the camp—he called it ‘the tent of meeting’. Then anyone who wanted a decision from Yahweh would go out to that tent of meeting outside the camp.
\v 8 Whenever Moses went out to the tent, all the people would stand up at the entrance of their own tents, and they would watch Moses go out until he went into the tent.
\v 9 Once he entered the tent, the pillar of cloud would come down and stand at the entrance of the tent, and Yahweh would speak with him.
\v 10 \add When\add* all the people saw the pillar of cloud standing at the entrance of the tent, they would get up and bow down at the entrance of their \add own\add* tents.
\v 11 Yahweh would speak to Moses face to face like someone speaks to their friend. Then Moses would return to the camp, but his servant Joshua a young man who was the son of Nun) would not leave the inside of the tent.
\s1 Moses sees the master’s splendour
\p
\v 12 Then Moses said to Yahweh, “Listen, you’ve been saying to me, ‘Take these people onwards,’ but you haven’t let me know who you’ll send with me, although you have said, ‘I know you by name, and you’ve also found favour in my sight.’
\v 13 So now please, if I’ve found favour in your sight, please help me to know your ways. Then I’ll know you so that I can find favour in your sight. And don’t forget that this nation is \em your\em* people.”
\p
\v 14 Yahweh replied, “My presence will go \add with you\add* and I will help you \add to be able to\add* rest.”
\p
\v 15 Moses replied to him, “If your presence isn’t coming with us, then don’t make us leave here.
\v 16 How would anyone know that I’ve found favour in your sight—me and your people \add that is\add*—if you didn’t come with us? How could I and your people be distinguished from all the \add other\add* people \add groups\add* in the world?”
\p
\v 17 Yahweh replied to Moses, “\add Okay,\add* I’ll also do what you’ve asked because you’ve found favour in my sight and I know you by name.”
\p
\v 18 Then Moses, “Please show me your splendour.”
\p
\v 19 Yahweh replied, “I myself will cause all my goodness to pass over in front of you, and I will say my name of Yahweh in front of you. I will favour anyone I want, and I’ll be compassionate to whoever I want.”
\v 20 But he said, “You won’t be able to see my face, because no human can see me and \add still\add* live.”
\v 21 He continued, “Look, there’s a place near me where you can stand there on the rock.
\v 22 Then while my splendour passes over, I’ll put you in a crevice in the rock, and my hand will cover you until I’ve gone past.
\v 23 Then I’ll take my hand away and you’ll be able to see my back, but you mustn’t try to look at my face.”
\c 34
\s1 The replacement stone slabs
\r (Deu. 10:1-5)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh continued speaking to Moses, “Carve two slabs of stone for yourself just like the first ones. Then the words that were on the first tablets that you broke, I’ll write them \add again\add* on the slabs.
\v 2 Be ready by morning, and in the morning come up to Mt. Sinai, and present yourself there to me up on the top.
\v 3 Don’t bring anyone with you, and also don’t let anyone be on the mountain at all—don’t even let their flocks and herds graze at the base of that mountain.”
\v 4 So Moses carved out two slabs of stone like the first ones, and he got up early in the morning and went up to Mt. Sinai as Yahweh had commanded him, carrying up the two slabs of stone.
\v 5 Then Yahweh came down in the cloud and stood with him there, and he pronounced his name ‘Yahweh’.
\v 6 Yahweh passed over above his face and declared, “I am Yahweh, a compassionate and gracious God, slow to get angry but exuding faithfulness and trustworthiness.
\v 7 \add I\add* display faithfulness to thousands \add of generations\add*—forgiving disobedience, transgression, and sin. But I certainly won’t let the guilty get away with it—visiting the iniquity of the parents onto the children and onto the grandchildren—to the third and the fourth \add generations\add*.”
\p
\v 8 Moses hurried and bowed to the ground and worshipped Yahweh
\v 9 and said, “Please, if I have found favour in your sight, my master, please go with us, because these are stubborn people. And forgive our disobedience and our sin and take us as your inheritance.”
\p
\v 10 Yahweh said, “Listen, I’m about to make an agreement in front of all your people.\f + \fr 34:10 \ft Because there’s no punctuation in the original Hebrew, it’s not certain if ‘in front of all your people’ goes with what it follows (as the \+em OET-RV\+em* has chosen), or with what follows it (as in several other translations).\f* I will do miracles that have not been done before anywhere on the earth or in any country \add of the world\add*. All the people that you’re among will see my work, because what I’m going to do with you is awe-inspiring.
\v 11 Be sure to obey what I’m telling you today. See, I’m about to force the Amorites, the Canaanites, the Hittites, the Perizim, the Hivim, and the Jebusites out of the land ahead of you.
\v 12 Be very careful not to make any treaties with any of the \add current\add* inhabitants of the land that you’re about to enter, in case it ends up trapping you.
\v 13 What you must do instead is to break down their altars and smash their pillars, and cut down the poles \add used to worship their god\add* Asherah.
\v 14 You must not bow down to any other god because Yahweh is a jealous God—jealous \add to protect\add* his name.
\v 15 So don’t make an agreement with the inhabitants of the land, \add because when\add* they prostitute themselves to their gods and sacrifice to them, and then they invite you \add to join them\add* and you eat some of their sacrifice,
\v 16 then \add you’ll end up by\add* allowing your sons to marry their daughters who’ll prostitute themselves to their gods, and then your sons will do the same.
\p
\v 17 Don’t make cast metal gods for yourselves.
\p
\v 18 You must observe the Flat Bread Celebration \add every year\add* as I instructed you. For seven days you must eat \add only\add* bread that hasn’t been risen, at the appointed time in late March or early April because that’s when you came out of Egypt.
\v 19 All of your eldest male offspring belong to me, \add including your sons\add* and the first males born from your cattle and sheep and goats.
\v 20 If it’s a male donkey, a lamb can be given as a ransom instead, but if you don’t pay a ransom then you must kill the donkey. It’s compulsory to ransom your eldest sons. Don’t come to worship me without bringing something.
\p
\v 21 \add Each week,\add* you should only work for six days, then you must rest on the seventh day—\add even\add* during ploughing and harvest times.
\p
\v 22 \add In the spring\add* when you begin to harvest the first wheat crop, you must have a \it Celebration of Weeks\it*, and at the end of the year \add in the autumn\add*, have a \it \+add Finished\+add* Harvest Celebration\it*.
\p
\v 23 Three times every year, all your men must present themselves before me—Israel’s god, the master Yahweh.
\v 24 I will drive out the people groups ahead of you and expand your borders, and no one will think of taking your land \add when they know that\add* you present yourselves to me three times a year.
\p
\v 25 Don’t \add wave bread\add* that’s been risen when offering a sacrifice. Don’t allow any part of the sacrifice for the ‘pass-over’ celebration to still be there in the morning.
\p
\v 26 You must bring the best of the first parts of your harvests to my residence.
\p Don’t boil a young goat in its mother’s milk.
\p
\v 27 Yahweh told Moses, “Write down these words for yourself, because it’s in accordance with these words that I’ve made an agreement with you and with Israel.”
\v 28 Moses was there with Yahweh for forty days and nights, and \add during that time\add* he didn’t eat or \add even\add* drink water, and he wrote the ten commands of the agreement on the \add stone\add* slabs.
\p
\v 29 So when Moses came down from Mt. Sinai, he was carrying the two \add stone\add* slabs with the transcript \add of the ten commands\add*, but what he didn’t know as he descended was that the skin on his face was shining after his talking with Yahweh.
\v 30 When Aaron and the Israelites saw Moses, wow, his face radiated light and they were afraid to go close to him.
\v 31 But Moses called them \add over\add*, and Aaron and all of the community leaders returned back to him, and he spoke with them.
\v 32 After that, the Israelites came closer, and Moses gave them all the instructions that Yahweh had told him on Mt. Sinai.
\v 33 Then when he’d finished talking to them, Moses put a veil over his face,
\v 34 and whenever he would go in to speak with Yahweh, he would remove the veil until he came out. Whenever he did come out, he’d tell the Israelites what he’d been instructed,
\v 35 and they would see how his face shone. Then Moses would put the veil back over his face until he went in \add the next time\add* to speak with Yahweh.
\c 35
\s1 Instructions for the rest day
\p
\v 1 Moses assembled all the Israelites and told them, “These are the things that Yahweh has instructed you to do.
\v 2 \add Every week\add*, you can work for six days, but the seventh day will be a sacred rest day, \add dedicated to\add* Yahweh. Anyone who works on that day must be put to death.
\v 3 Don’t light a fire\f + \fr 35:3 \ft This is effectively a command not to do any \+bd cooking\+bd* on the rest day.\f* in any of your homes on the rest days.”
\s1 The offering for Yahweh
\r (Exo. 25:1-9)
\p
\v 4 Then Moses told all the assembled Israelites, “This is what Yahweh instructed:
\v 5 Take up an offering for Yahweh—everyone who wants to participate should bring to Yahweh their contribution of gold, silver, bronze,
\v 6 blue, purple, or scarlet yarn, finely-spun linen and goats’ hair,
\v 7 reddened ram skins, sea-cow hides, and acacia wood,
\v 8 \add olive\add* oil for the lamps and spices to make the anointing oil and the fragrant incense,
\v 9 and onyx and other gemstones for the \add priest’s\add* sacred apron and chest pouch.
\s1 The equipment for the holy tent
\r (Exo. 39:32-43)
\p
\v 10 Everyone among you all who’s highly skilled should come and make everything that Yahweh has instructed—
\v 11 the tent for the residence and its covering, its clasps and frames, its bars, pillars, and bases,
\v 12 the box with its poles and the atonement platform \add to go on top\add*, the curtain for screening it,
\v 13 the table and its poles and all its utensils and the \add sacred\add* bread,
\v 14 the lampstand for light with its lamps and utensils and oil to burn,
\v 15 the incense altar and its poles, the anointing oil and fragrant incense, the curtain for the entrance of the residence,
\v 16 the altar for the burnt offerings with its bronze grate, its poles and all of its utensils, the washbasin with its base,
\v 17 the courtyard curtains with their pillars and bases, and the curtain for the courtyard gate,
\v 18 the tent pegs and ropes for the residence and the courtyard,
\v 19 the finely-woven clothing for serving in the Holy Place, the sacred clothes for Aaron the priest, and the clothes for his sons to serve as priests.”
\s1 The people’s offerings
\p
\v 20 Then all the Israelites who’d been assembled in front of Moses went away,
\v 21 and everyone who felt to do so came \add back\add* bringing their contributions to Yahweh for the work on the sacred tent and all its components, and for the sacred clothes.
\v 22 That included both men and women who were wanting to contribute, and they brought necklaces and earrings and ornaments—all made of gold. They held them up high as a raised offering of gold to Yahweh.
\v 23 Many people who had blue or purple or scarlet yarn, or finely-spun linen or goats’ hair, reddened ram skins or sea-cow hides brought them.
\v 24 Others who could, brought a contribution of silver or bronze to Yahweh, and those with acacia wood brought it for all the construction of the tent components.
\v 25 All the women who were skilled at making cloth brought blue, purple, or scarlet yarn and fine, hand-spun linen thread,
\v 26 and all those women who felt to do so spun goats’ hair.
\v 27 The leaders brought onyx stones and other gemstones to be mounted on the sacred apron and for the sacred pouch,
\v 28 along with the spices and \add olive\add* oil to be used for the maps, the anointing oil, and the fragrant incense.
\v 29 \add Thus\add* the Israelites brought a voluntary offering to Yahweh—every man and woman who felt to do so brought something for all the work which Yahweh had instructed them through Moses to do.
\s1 The skills of Betsalel and Oholiav
\r (Exo. 31:1-11)
\p
\v 30 Then Moses said to the Israelites, “Listen, Yahweh has called Betsalel by name (the son of Hur’s son Uri, from the Judah tribe)
\v 31 and he has filled him with his spirit, with skill and understanding, and with knowledge and with all kinds of craftsmanship.
\v 32 \add He can\add* design designs, work in gold, silver, and bronze,
\v 33 in cutting gemstones for setting, and in wood-carving—to do all kinds of design and crafting.
\v 34 Yahweh has also given him the desire to teach, along with Oholiav (Ahisamak’s son from the Dan tribe).
\v 35 He’s given them \add both\add* skills to do all the work of craftsmen and of skillful workmen and of embroiderers in blue, purple, and scarlet yarn, and in the fine linen and of weavers—\add both\add* as designers as well as the various, \add actual\add* crafts.
\c 36
\nb
\v 1 Betsalel and Oholiav will do the work of constructing all the components of the sacred tent along with any others who are like-minded and who have been given the necessary skills and understanding by Yahweh.”
\s1 More than enough materials donated
\p
\v 2 So Moses summoned Betsalel and Oholiav, as well as anyone else who felt to help and had been given the \add appropriate\add* skills by Yahweh,
\v 3 and they took \add away\add* all the contributions that the Israelites had brought to Moses to be used for the construction of the sacred tent. However the people \add still\add* continued to bring voluntary offerings every morning.
\v 4 So all those skilled \add people\add* came \add over\add* to \add start on\add* the work of \add making\add* the sacred tent, Holy Place came, leaving behind the work that they had been doing,
\v 5 and they informed Moses, “The people are bringing way more than enough to use for the work that Yahweh has instructed us to do.”
\p
\v 6 So with Moses’s agreement, they spread a message around the camp saying, “Please don’t bring any more contributions for the sacred tent.” So the people—men and women—stopped bringing donations
\v 7 as \add they had already collected\add* more than enough materials for all the work.
\s1 The construction of the tent
\r (Exo. 26:1-37)
\p
\v 8 Then all the most-skilled men out of the workmen made the ten \add strips of\add* curtaining for the residence from twisted finely-spun linen and blue, purple, scarlet yarn \add used to embroider\add* winged-creatures on them.
\v 9 Each strip was 14.5m long and two metres wide—they were all the same size.
\v 10 Five strips were sewn together as one set, and the other five as another set.
\v 11 They\f + \fr 36:11 \ft It’s singular ‘he’ in the original, which is confusing after v8 mentioned a group of craftsmen. The \+em OET-RV\+em* has chosen to follow v8 (and change the pronoun to ‘they’ in this section), but other translations are divided in how they handle this dilemma.\f* made loops of blue on the edge of the first curtain at the edge of the set, and similarly for the second set—
\v 12 fifty \add matching\add* loops on the edge of each set so they were aligned with each other.
\v 13 They \add also\add* made fifty gold clips and used them to join the \add two\add* curtain \add sets\add* to each other to make \add the wall of\add* the sacred tent.
\p
\v 14 Then they made eleven goats’ hair curtain sections for the tarpaulin over the sacred tent.
\v 15 All eleven sections were the same size at fifteen metres long and two metres wide,
\v 16 and they joined five sections to each other \add for one set\add* and six for the other.
\v 17 Then they attached fifty \add blue\add* loops onto one edge of each of the two sets
\v 18 and made fifty bronze clips for connecting them into one \add continuous\add* cover.
\v 19 They then made a tarpaulin from red rams skins to go over the tent, and the top \add waterproof\add* tarpaulin of sea-cow hides.
\p
\v 20 Then they made \add forty-eight\add* upright frames from acacia wood for the sacred tent.
\v 21 Each frame was five metres long and 0.75m wide
\v 22 with two protrusions on each frame for connecting them together, so all the frames were made in this way.
\v 23 They made twenty frames for the south side
\v 24 with their forty silver bases—two bases under each frame that two \add more\add* protrusions fitted \add down\add* into,
\v 25 then two \add more\add* for the north side
\v 26 with their forty silver bases—two bases under each frame.
\v 27 Six frames were made for the west side
\v 28 as well as two frames for the back corners of the sacred tent.
\v 29 At the two corners, the frames were connected together at the bottom, and \add similarly\add* at the top before being attached to a single ring
\v 30 so that made eight frames with their sixteen silver bases—two bases under each frame.
\p
\v 31 Then they made crossbars from acacia wood—five for the frames on the \add north\add* side of the sacred tent,
\v 32 five for the south side, and five crossbars for the frames on the west \add side\add* along the back.
\v 33 The middle crossbar was made to pass through from \add one\add* end to the other in the centre of the frames,
\v 34 and the frames were overlaid with gold. They then made gold rings to be the holders for the crossbars, and they \add too\add* were overlaid with gold.
\p
\v 35 They made a curtain from twisted finely-spun linen, and a skillful workman embroidered winged creatures on them with blue, purple, scarlet yarn.
\v 36 They made four pillars of acacia wood overlaid with gold, as well as their gold hooks and four bases cast from silver.
\v 37 They made a curtain for the tent entrance—an embroiderer made it from twisted finely-spun linen and blue, purple, and scarlet yarn,
\v 38 along with its five pillars and their hooks. The tops of the pillars were overlaid with gold, and their five bases \add were made of\add* bronze.
\c 37
\s1 Making the sacred box
\r (Exo. 25:10-22)
\p
\v 1 Then Betsalel made the \add sacred\add* chest from acacia wood—it was 1.2m long, 0.75m wide, and 0.75m high.
\v 2 He overlaid \add both\add* the inside and the outside with pure gold, and made a gold moulding all around it,
\v 3 and he cast four rings of gold \add and attached them\add* to its four feet—two rings on each one side.
\v 4 He made \add two\add* acacia wood poles and overlaid them with gold,
\v 5 and he put the poles into the rings on the sides of the box for carrying it.
\p
\v 6 Then he made the atonement platform from pure gold \add to fit\add* as the lid for the box, \add so\add* it was \add also\add* 1.2m long and 0.75m wide,
\v 7 then from the ends of the lid, he hammered the gold to form two winged creatures.
\v 8 There was a winged creature sitting on each end of the atonement platform made from its two ends,
\v 9 and they were spreading their wings upwards to cover over the platform. They faced each other, looking down towards the atonement platform.
\s1 Making the bread table
\r (Exo. 25:23-30)
\p
\v 10 He made \add the bread\add* table from acacia wood—one metre long, half a metre wide, and three-quarters of a metre high.
\v 11 It was overlaid with pure gold and had a gold moulding all around it.
\v 12 He made an 8cm wide rim all around it, and a gold moulding around the rim.
\v 13 He moulded four gold rings from gold and attached them to the four corners of the table. Each ring was close to each table leg
\v 14 near the rim to hold the poles for carrying the table.
\v 15 He made \add two\add* acacia wood poles and overlaid them for gold—they were for carrying the table.
\v 16 He \add also\add* made the utensils that would be on the table—its plates and pans, and its bowls and pitchers to be used for pouring out the wine offerings—all made from pure gold.
\s1 Making the lampstand
\r (Exo. 25:31-40)
\p
\v 17 Then he made the lampstand from pure gold. The base and shaft of the lampstand were hammered into shape, along with its cups, and its buds and blossoms.
\v 18 There were six branches going out from the shaft of the lampstand—three on each side.
\v 19 Each branch had three gold cups on it that looked like almond flowers with buds and petals. It was the same for each of the lampstand’s six branches.
\v 20 On the lampstand \add shaft\add* there were four cups that also looked like almond blossoms, each one with flower buds and petals.
\v 21 He made one flower bud beneath every pair of branches \add (that were going out from opposite sides)\add*. The bud was attached to each pair of branches as if they were growing out of it—all six lampstand branches were attached like that.
\v 22 The buds and the branches coming out of them were all hammered out of one piece of pure gold.
\v 23 Then he made the seven lamps out of pure gold, along with the tongs and trays.
\v 24 He made the lampstand and tongs and trays using 33 kilograms of pure gold.
\s1 Making the incense altar
\r (Exo. 30:1-5)
\p
\v 25 Then he made the altar \add for burning\add* incense out of acacia wood. It was square—half a metre on each side—and one metre high. Its projections \add on the top four corners\add* were part of it.
\v 26 He overlaid it with pure gold—all four sides including the projections—and put a gold moulding all around it.
\v 27 Then he made two gold rings \add and attached them\add* below the moulding—two on each side for holding the poles for carrying the altar.
\v 28 The \add two\add* poles were made from acacia wood and then overlaid with gold.
\v 29 He got a \add professional\add* ointment maker to make the sacred anointing oil and the pure, fragrant incense.
\c 38
\s1 Making the altar for burning sacrifices
\r (Exo. 27:1-8)
\p
\v 1 Then Betsalel made the altar for burning offerings from acacia wood. It was square—2.5m on each side—and 1.5m tall.
\v 2 From the same piece of wood, he made horn-like projections for each of the four corners, then he overlaid it all with bronze.
\v 3 He made all the utensils for the altar: the pots and shovels, basins and meat-forks, and the firepans—all made from bronze.
\v 4 He made a grating for the altar—a bronze lattice \add that fitted\add* down in the middle of the altar,
\v 5 and he cast the grating with rings on the four corners as holders for the \add carrying\add* poles.
\v 6 He made the acacia wood poles and overlaid them with bronze
\v 7 and he put the poles into the rings on the sides of the altar so they could carry it with them. He made the altar hollow using boards.
\s1 Making the washbasin
\r (Exo. 30:18)
\p
\v 8 Then he made a bronze washbasin and base. \add They were made\add* with the bronze from the mirrors of the serving women who served at the entrance of the \add sacred\add* tent.
\s1 Making the courtyard
\r (Exo. 27:9-19)
\p
\v 9 Then Betsalel worked on the courtyard. For the south side, the courtyard curtains were fifty metres of twisted finely-spun linen
\v 10 with their twenty \add bronze\add* pillars and their bases. The hooks on the pillars and their rings were made of silver.
\v 11 On the north side, there were fifty metres of curtains with their twenty bronze pillars and bases, and their silver hooks and rings.
\v 12 On the west side, there were twenty-five metres of curtains with their ten pillars and bases, and their silver hooks and rings,
\v 13 and \add another\add* twenty-five metres on the east side.
\v 14 The curtains for \add one\add* side \add of the entrance\add* were 7.5m long with their three pillars and their bases.
\v 15 On the other side, opposite the courtyard entrance, the curtains were 7.5m long with their three pillars and their bases.
\v 16 All of the courtyard curtains were made with twisted finely-spun linen.
\v 17 The bases for the pillars were bronze, but the hooks on them and their rings were silver. All of the courtyard pillars had decorative silver caps fitted to their tops.
\v 18 The curtain for the courtyard gate was done by an embroiderer. It was made from twisted finely-spun linen \add embroidered\add* with blue, purple, and scarlet yard. The curtain was ten metres long and 2.5m high, just like the other courtyard curtains
\v 19 with its four bronze pillars and their bases and their silver hooks and rings, and with silver caps.
\v 20 All the tent pegs for the sacred tent and for around the courtyard were made with bronze.
\s1 List of metals used
\p
\v 21 These are the records \add from making\add* the sacred tent (where the \add stone\add* transcripts were kept). They were recorded by the Levites according to Moses’ instructions \add and supervised by\add* Itamar (a son of Aaron the priest).
\p
\v 22 Betsalel (son of Uri son of Hur, from the Judah tribe) made everything that Yahweh had instructed Moses to make.
\v 23 Oholiav (son of Ahisamak, from the Dan tribe) worked with him—he was an engraver and a designer and an embroiderer in blue, purple, and scarlet, and in fine linen.
\p
\v 24 The gold that they used for the construction of the sacred tent was measured at 965 kilograms. This was the gold from the wave offering and was weighed using the official standard weights.
\v 25 The silver contributed by the people was measured at 3,420 kilograms \add also\add* using the official weights.
\v 26 \add During the census,\add* every male who was twenty years old or older paid a 5g silver coin—a total of 603,550 men.
\v 27 They’d used 3,400 kilograms of silver to make the bases \add under the pillars that supported\add* the sacred tent’s curtains (34 kilograms for each of one hundred bases)
\v 28 and the other twenty kilograms were used for the hooks for the pillars and for the caps on the tops of them.
\v 29 The bronze from the wave offering came to 2,400 kilograms
\v 30 and it was used to make the bases \add for the pillars\add* at the tent entrance and for the altar and its grating and all the utensils,
\v 31 and for the bases \add for the posts\add* for \add the curtains\add* around the courtyard and at the entrance, and for the tent pegs for the main structure and around the courtyard.
\c 39
\s1 Making the priests’ uniforms
\r (Exo. 28:1-14)
\p
\v 1 They used the blue, purple, and scarlet \add cloth\add* to make the sacred clothes to be used in the sacred tent. They made Aaron’s clothes just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\s2 The sacred apron
\p
\v 2 Betsalel made the sacred apron from twisted finely-spun linen \add embroidered with\add* gold \add wire\add*, and blue, purple, and scarlet yarn.
\v 3 They hammered thin sheets of gold and he cut them into thread of wire for a skillful craftsman to embroider into the fine linen along with the blue, purple, and scarlet yarn.
\v 4 They made joined shoulder pieces for it—joined on two corners
\v 5 and the skillfully woven waistband which is attached to the apron was made of the same material of twisted finely-spun linen, and had similar embroidery using gold \add wire\add*, and blue, purple, and scarlet yarn, just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\v 6 They cut \add two\add* onyx stones and mounted them in decorative gold settings. The names of the \add twelve\add* sons of Israel were engraved on \add them\add* similar to how a signet ring is engraved,
\v 7 and he put them on the shoulder pieces of the sacred apron as stones of reminder for the sons of Israel, as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\s1 Making the sacred pouch
\r (Exo. 28:15-30)
\p
\v 8 Then he made the \add sacred\add* chest pouch which took the work of a skilled craftsman like making the sacred apron. It too was made of twisted finely-spun linen and embroidered with gold \add wire\add*, and blue, purple, and scarlet yarn.
\v 9 When the material was folded over double, it made a square pouch that was 23cm long and wide,
\v 10 and they attached four rows of gemstones onto the front. The first row had a \add red\add* ruby, a \add yellow\add* topaz, and a \add red\add* garnet.
\v 11 The second row had a \add green\add* emerald, a \add blue\add* sapphire, and a diamond.
\v 12 The third row had a \add red\add* jacinth, a \add white\add* agate, and a \add purple\add* amethyst.
\v 13 The fourth row had a \add yellow\add* beryl, an onyx, and a \add green\add* jasper. All the stones were mounted in decorative gold settings.
\v 14 The gemstones were engraved with the names of the twelve sons of Israel similar to how a signet ring is engraved, and they represented the twelve tribes.
\v 15 They \add attached two\add* twisted chains of pure gold crafted like a cord onto the pouch,
\v 16 and made two decorative gold settings and two gold rings, and attached them to the upper corners of the sacred pouch.
\v 17 They attached one end of each gold chain to one of the rings on the top corner of the pouch,
\v 18 and they attached the other end of each chain to the two decorative settings that enclose the stones. Then they put those on the front side of the shoulder straps of the sacred apron.
\v 19 Then they made two \add more\add* gold rings and attached them to the lower corners of the sacred pouch on the inside edges next to the sacred apron.
\v 20 They made two more gold rings and attached them to the lower part of the front of the shoulder straps near where the shoulder straps join with the sacred apron just above the waistband.
\v 21 They tied the rings on the sacred pouch to the rings on the sacred apron with a blue cord, so that the sacred pouch was above the waistband and would not come loose from the apron. They did those things exactly as Yahweh had instructed Moses to do.
\s1 Making the other clothes
\r (Exo. 28:31-43)
\p
\v 22 Betsalel had a weaver make the robe \add that would be worn under\add* the sacred apron entirely from blue \add material\add*.
\v 23 It opened down the middle like other clothes and had a border around that opening so that it wouldn’t tear,
\v 24 and the bottom hem had \add decorative\add* pomegranates on it that were made from blue, purple, and scarlet yarn.
\v 25 They \add also\add* made \add small\add* bells from pure gold and attached them between the pomegranates around the bottom of the robe,
\v 26 so it alternated between a bell and a pomegranate going around the hem, just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\p
\v 27 A weaver made tunics for Aaron and his sons from fine linen,
\v 28 along with the turban and caps from fine linen, the underwear made from twisted finely-spun linen,
\v 29 and the waistband from fine linen with blue, purple, and scarlet embroidery, just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\p
\v 30 They made a \add decorative\add* plate (to represent a holy crown) from pure gold and they engraved ‘Dedicated to Yahweh’ on it (like you’d do for a signet ring).
\v 31 They put a blue cord on it to attach it to the front of the turban, just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\s1 Moses inspects the finished components
\r (Exo. 35:10-19)
\p
\v 32 And \add so\add* all the components of the residence, the tent for meeting God, were finished—the Israelites had done just what Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\v 33 The craftsmen brought the \add disassembled\add* residence to Moses—the tent and all its equipment, the clips, the frames with their bars, and the pillars with their bases,
\v 34 the reddened rams hide tarpaulin and the top sea-cow hide tarpaulin, the curtain for covering the most sacred place,
\v 35 the box for the transcript slabs and its poles, the atonement platform,
\v 36 the table with all its utensils and the \add sacred\add* bread to present to God,
\v 37 the pure lampstand with its row of lamps and all its utensils and the lamp oil,
\v 38 the gold \add incense\add* altar, the anointing oil and the fragrant incense, and the curtain for the tent entrance,
\v 39 the \add large\add* bronze altar with its grating, its poles, and all its utensils, the washbasin with its base,
\v 40 the courtyard curtains with their pillars and bases, the courtyard gate curtain, \add all the\add* ropes and tent pegs and all the items to service the residence as the tent of meeting,
\v 41 and the finely-woven clothes for serving in the holy place, the sacred clothes for Aaron the priest, and the clothes for his sons to \add serve as\add* priests.
\p
\v 42 The Israelites had done all the work just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\v 43 Moses inspected all the work, and wow, they had done it just as Yahweh had instructed them, and Moses blessed them.
\c 40
\s1 Erecting the sacred tent
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “On the first day of the first month, you should set up the tent where we’ll meet.
\v 3 Put the box for the transcript slabs in its place and \add hang\add* the curtain to screen it \add from sight\add*.
\v 4 Then bring in the \add bread\add* table and arrange its utensils, and bring in the lampstand and set up the lamps,
\v 5 and put the gold incense altar in front of the \add sacred\add* box and set up the entrance curtain for the tent.
\v 6 Put the altar for burning sacrifices in front of the entrance to the sacred tent where we’ll meet.
\v 7 Put the washbasin between the \add sacred\add* tent and that altar, and fill it with water.
\v 8 Set-up the curtains around the outside to make the courtyard, and also set up the courtyard’s entrance curtain.
\p
\v 9 Then take the \add sacred\add* anointing oil and smear it on the sacred tent and everything that’s in it, then none of those things should be touched by others—it will \add all\add* be sacred.
\v 10 Also anoint the altar for burnt offerings and all its utensils. After that, the altar shouldn’t be touched by others—it will be \em very\em* sacred.
\v 11 Anoint the washbasin and its base, and then prohibit others from using it.
\p
\v 12 Now bring Aaron and his sons to the entrance of the \add sacred\add* tent and wash them with water.
\v 13 Then put the sacred garments on Aaron and anoint him and make him sacred, so he can \add now\add* serve as a priest to me.
\v 14 Also bring his sons and dress them in tunics
\v 15 and anoint them like you anointed their father, and they will serve as priests to me. Their anointing will cause them to be a priesthood forever throughout their generations.”
\p
\v 16 So Moses did \add everything\add* that Yahweh had instructed him to do.
\v 17 It was the first day of the first month of their second year when they set up the sacred tent.
\v 18 Moses set up the residence by setting out its bases and standing up the frames with their bars, and the pillars.
\v 19 He spread the two tarpaulin layers over the tent just as Yahweh had instructed him.
\v 20 Then he took the \add two stone slabs with the\add* transcript and put them into the box. He put the poles \add into the rings\add* on the box and fitted the atonement platform on the top of the box \add as a lid\add*,
\v 21 and brought the box into the residence. He set up the curtain to conceal the box with the transcript, just as Yahweh had instructed him.
\p
\v 22 He put the table into the sacred tent on the north side of the residence, outside the curtain,
\v 23 and arranged the bread on it to display it to Yahweh, just as Yahweh had instructed him.
\v 24 He put the lampstand inside the sacred tent across from the table, on the south side of the residence,
\v 25 and he placed the lamps on it in Yahweh’s presence, just as Yahweh had instructed him.
\v 26 Then he put the gold \add incense\add* altar inside the \add sacred\add* tent in front of the curtain \add that screened the sacred box from view\add*,
\v 27 and burnt some fragrant incense on it, just as Yahweh had instructed him,
\v 28 then he hung the curtain at the tent entrance.
\v 29 He put the altar for burning sacrifices at the gate of the sacred tent and offered the burnt offering and the grain offering up on it, just as Yahweh had instructed him.
\v 30 Then he placed the washbasin between the tent and the altar, and filled it with water
\v 31 so that Moses, and Aaron and his sons, could wash their hands and feet from it.
\v 32 Whenever they went into the \add sacred\add* tent or whenever they went near the altar, \add first\add* they would wash themselves, just as Yahweh had commanded Moses.
\v 33 He erected the courtyard \add curtains\add* around the residence and the altar, and he set up the courtyard gate curtain. And \add so\add* Moses finished the work.
\s1 Yahweh’s splendour fills the tent
\r (Num. 9:15-23)
\p
\v 34 Then a cloud covered the sacred tent and Yahweh’s power and bright light filled the residence
\v 35 and Moses was unable to enter the tent because the cloud had settled on it and Yahweh’s splendour had filled the residence.
\v 36 \add From then onwards,\add* whenever the cloud went up from over the residence, the Israelites would \add pack their camp and\add* set out on their travels,
\v 37 but while the cloud remained, they would stay in that place until the cloud lifted.
\v 38 Yahweh’s cloud would be there during the day but it would become fire at night, and so the Israelites could always see it.
